WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:11.460
- This next section,

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.980
we're gonna be focused
on modern SOF strategy.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.510
Really, we're gonna
build off the framework

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:18.000
that DASD Logan presented
during our keynote.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.070
Additionally, we're
gonna have Peter Bergen,

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:22.440
who is a senior CNN News analyst,

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:25.380
to moderate the presentation
for our four panelists.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:27.330
We're gonna have about 50 minutes for them

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:29.520
to engage in conversation.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:32.250
Additionally, we'll have
about 15, 20 minutes of Q&amp;A.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:33.600
Again, please think about some questions.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.633
We want this to be interactive.

00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:36.990
We want some provocative thoughts,

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:38.010
some ideas to flow.

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:41.520
So think about that as Peter
moderates this conversation.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:42.353
Thank you.

00:00:42.353 --> 00:00:43.380
- Thank you, Mike.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.660
Also, we have an all-star panel.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.440
Directly to my left is Colonel Jewell,

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:50.310
who's the director

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:53.540
of the Adaptive Innovative
Statecraft Unit in

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:57.150
at JSOU multiple combat tours in Iraq.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:00.360
Next to Colonel Jewell
is Colonel Hooker who,

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:01.193
amongst other things,

00:01:01.193 --> 00:01:03.690
was senior director for Europe and Russia

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.030
at the NSC between 2017 and 2018.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:08.213
Kind of an interesting
time to be doing that.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.870
And also was the Teddy
Roosevelt chair at NDU.

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:16.740
He's now a senior fellow
at the Atlantic Council.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:18.630
Next to him is Ben Milligan,

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:22.080
who was on SEAL Team 4 and 5.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.587
He just wrote this book,

00:01:23.587 --> 00:01:26.370
"By Water Beneath the Walls:
The Rise of the Navy SEALs".

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:28.890
I read 150 pages of of it last night.

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:29.970
I could not put it down.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:31.500
It is so good.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.750
Took him seven years to write.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:35.370
And next to him is Colonel Collins,

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:39.630
who is the executive director
of the Viola Foundation,

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:43.920
the editor of "Routledge
handbook of U.S. Counterterrorism

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:44.790
and Irregular Warfare",

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:48.780
which is absolutely massive
and authoritative book,

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.730
which was done in the
memory of Mike Sheehan

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.257
who was ASD-SOLIC.

00:01:52.257 --> 00:01:55.320
And probably some of
you now remember Mike,

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:58.740
he is also the co-author of
"Understanding Urban Warfare",

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:02.703
which I think is gonna be a
useful discussion point here.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:04.723
And he used

00:02:04.723 --> 00:02:07.650
to direct the Counterterrorism
Center at West Point.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:11.550
So I'm just gonna start
with Colonel Hooker,

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:12.450
if you don't mind,

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:15.173
because you wrote

00:02:15.173 --> 00:02:18.033
what I thought was a
rather provocative piece,

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:22.980
which you can describe perhaps

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.680
and give us the, I would say,

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.190
a blistering critique
of Special Operations

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:31.563
as they are presently constructed.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:33.543
So go ahead.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:35.613
- Well, okay.

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:37.703
Thanks for that.

00:02:37.703 --> 00:02:40.440
I guess still kind of
wondering why I was invited

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.090
to this conference.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:42.960
I wanna lead off

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:46.680
by saying what I said in
my JFQ article up front,

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.300
which is that, undeniably,

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:51.570
special operating forces have a key role

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:54.210
to play in national security.

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:57.450
Undeniably, special operating
forces have a key role

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.160
to play in national security.

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.230
I wanna put that up front.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:03.720
But I hope to inject some balance

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.250
into the conversation today.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.290
And I hope I don't trigger
anybody in the room,

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:10.710
but maybe that wouldn't be
such a bad thing after all.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:12.390
I'm gonna give you a perspective

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.280
from outside the SOF community,

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:16.470
from the conventional community,

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:20.580
but also I think from the
policymaking point of view,

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.180
which is informed by, I guess,

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:26.580
my three tours on the
National Security Council.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:32.450
So let's talk a little bit
about some of the trends

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.610
in the SOF community really since 9/11.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:39.210
So the first is a real explosion in size.

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:44.143
So SOF doubled in size from
9/11 to the present day.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:46.227
How did it do that?

00:03:46.227 --> 00:03:49.500
The 4th Battalion was
added to each SF group.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.210
Along with a special troops battalion,

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:52.740
military intelligence battalion added

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:54.480
to the 75th Ranger Regiment,

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:57.210
which also added to 4th Rifle Company

00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:59.280
and his support company in each battalion

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:00.573
and on and on and on.

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:04.590
I've got a document here that's put out

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:06.150
by the Assistant Secretary of Defense

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:08.790
for Manpower and Reserve Affairs,

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:10.410
which I found really interesting

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:12.030
at doing the research for the article.

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:14.370
So I was kind of dumbfounded to read

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:19.080
that where the joint staff
has 2,390 folks assigned,

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:21.840
and CENTCOM has 1,466.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:23.223
This was in 2020.

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:27.183
USSOCOM had 6,194.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:29.490
Well, now when you push this forward

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:32.040
to the most current document,

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.623
which is an estimate of F-22,

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:38.520
the numbers are about the
same for the joint staff,

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:39.840
for the other combatant commands.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:44.220
SOCOM comes in at 73,923,

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.233
which I think is the entire community.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:50.100
So why that methodology
is applied to this number

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.540
into no one else?

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:53.010
I'm not sure,

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:54.990
but there is an answer
out there somewhere.

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:58.290
It might be to obscure the
size of the SOCOM staff.

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.810
It's not clear to me why
the SOCOM staff needs

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:03.840
to be exponentially larger
than everybody else,

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.840
but maybe somebody in the room
can help me out with that.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:09.573
So a big explosion in size.

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:13.020
So the obvious kind of
question to that is,

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:15.660
how do you maintain the
quality of the force

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.450
when you've doubled in size?

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:20.430
So, we'll get into that a little bit later

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:21.830
in my presentation, I think.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.950
Dazi told us that SOF forces,

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:28.087
when compared to everyone else,

00:05:28.087 --> 00:05:29.430
are not putting words in your mouth.

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:30.330
They're cheap,

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:32.460
2% or whatever, whatever.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.530
Well, they're pretty expensive actually

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:36.990
because a lot of the costs
are hidden in service budget.

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:39.000
So manpower costs, for example, is one,

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.820
but many acquisition costs as well.

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:44.610
Even so, SOCOM's budget requests

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:45.900
for the current year was larger

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:48.630
than the entire defense budget of Poland,

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:51.333
which is NATO's largest
and strongest military,

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:53.910
at least until Ukraine gets in,

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:55.960
which hopefully won't be too much longer.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:05:59.340
So that's an interesting statistic.

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:02.550
So comes inventory of 30AC-130J aircraft

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:05.790
that comes in at 165 million each.

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:07.530
So that's more than an F-22,

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:09.840
that's more than an F-35.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:10.920
And this is an aircraft

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:13.440
that can only fly in
permissive environments.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.570
It's typically only used at night

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:16.710
and is almost never used

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:18.210
in support of conventional units,

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:20.460
even in extremist.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:24.450
Another example might be
the AT-802U light aircraft,

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:26.970
which is a modified crop duster

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:28.350
that SOCOM attempts to use

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:32.040
for close air support and armed overwatch.

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:34.240
And this program will
come in at$ 3 billion.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:39.030
I mean, is that a
example of an acquisition

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:40.260
that's gonna make a lot of sense

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:42.360
in great power competition
in high intensity,

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:43.320
major theater war?

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:44.733
Not so sure about that.

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:48.270
I would say paradoxically,

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:50.310
although expected to conduct operations

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:53.730
with the highest levels of
discipline and discretion,

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:55.890
in fact, a disproportionate number

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:57.390
of the most egregious mishaps

00:06:57.390 --> 00:07:01.260
in the war on terror befell SOF units.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:03.480
And I'm not gonna give
you a long laundry list,

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:05.850
although I have one if you're interested.

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:08.010
But if you picked a military disaster

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:12.510
from 2001 up to about 2018 or so,

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:14.100
there's about an 80% chance

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:15.450
that it involved the SOF unit,

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:16.893
not a conventional unit.

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:20.700
And I think that calls for some inquiry

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:22.950
into why that might be.

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:24.210
In fact, there was an inquiry

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:26.940
that was commissioned
by General Clark in 2019

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:29.280
after some congressional pressure,

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:30.750
which resulted in some findings,

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:32.773
which I thought were interesting.

00:07:32.773 --> 00:07:34.860
But not sure if it got
to the bottom of that.

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:35.700
It may well be,

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:37.100
and you all might know better than me,

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:40.080
it may have something to do with OPTEMPO.

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:41.700
We can have a conversation about that.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:43.857
It may have something
to do with the explosion

00:07:43.857 --> 00:07:46.020
and size of the force.

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:48.180
It may have something to
do with the connectivity

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:51.030
or lack of connectivity
with battlespace owners.

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:52.893
There's a lot to unpack there.

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:55.623
But I think this is significant.

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:01.860
I think a major contributing factor

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:04.230
to what we're talking about
here is the strong tendency

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:05.220
to wall off

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:08.910
or to stove pipe Special
Operations Forces.

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:13.110
So I'm gonna throw a thesis
out on the table here,

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:15.270
which I believe is sort of an iron law

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:16.470
of bureaucratic politics,

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:18.510
which is that all organizations,

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:20.040
Boy Scouts of America,

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:22.140
First Presbyterian Church of Dothan,

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:23.730
or USSOCOM,

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:25.560
they all want two things.

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:27.990
They want the maximum amount of autonomy

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:29.730
and they want the maximum
amount of resources

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:31.380
that they could get.

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:33.180
Now, some organizations prosper

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:34.830
and are super effective at this,

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:37.170
Marine Corps is an example
that comes to mind,

00:08:37.170 --> 00:08:39.270
and some are less successful.

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:41.310
I would venture to say

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:45.510
that the USSOCOM is
spectacularly successful at this.

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:46.920
So put yourself in the shoes

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:51.330
of General Petraeus in 2007 at MNF-I.

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:56.330
And the poor guy has got direct
control over Army forces,

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:59.400
but the Marines are not
task organized under him.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:01.800
The Air Force is not
task organized under him

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:03.420
as a theater commander.

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:06.480
Black SOF is not task organized under him.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:10.050
And white SOF is task organized
at the four star level,

00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:11.580
which means for tactical actions

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:14.124
that are going on every single day,

00:09:14.124 --> 00:09:15.690
there is no relationship there.

00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:18.390
You can't monitor or manage
those at the four star level,

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:19.293
you just can't.

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:21.720
So if you're a battle space owner,

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:22.553
I'm sorry,

00:09:22.553 --> 00:09:24.360
you're gonna have a lot of
SOF units running around

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:25.620
in your battle space.

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:27.660
And I can tell you this
from personal experience

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:29.400
as a unit commander

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:31.260
and five different campaigns.

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:33.660
And you're not gonna
know that they're there.

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:34.710
And maybe it's okay

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:36.720
and maybe it's not gonna be okay.

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:38.640
So that's a problem.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:41.970
So if we believe that unity
of effort is important,

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:45.150
then I submit we need to strive
for true unity of effort,

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:47.670
which means let's get a line
synchronized, integrated,

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:49.800
and coordinated on the battlefield.

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:52.590
Let's not set up a huge wall

00:09:52.590 --> 00:09:56.280
so that we can be as
autonomous as we want to be.

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:57.750
And I know I'm being provocative here,

00:09:57.750 --> 00:10:00.750
but I think this is a conversation
that needs to take place.

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:03.570
H. R. McMaster has written at some length

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:08.010
about the rating strategy
that was carried out

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:11.250
by SOF forces all all
through the campaigns

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:12.720
in Iraq and Afghanistan,

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:15.030
which in a real way tended to work

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:17.760
against the rest of the theater strategy.

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:18.690
So if what we're trying

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:21.000
to do is protect the civilian population

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:23.760
and win the civilian
population over to our side,

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:26.550
which is a tentative of counterinsurgency,

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:29.430
then constantly traumatizing
the civilian population

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:30.960
with night raids,

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:32.280
some of which were successful,

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:34.140
but many of which were not,

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:38.130
tended to work at counter
purposes to the theater strategy.

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:39.183
It really did.

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:42.630
And I think this is something
that we need to learn from

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:46.830
and think about as we
project the role of SOF

00:10:46.830 --> 00:10:48.990
into the next era,

00:10:48.990 --> 00:10:52.590
which is gonna certainly
feature some elements

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:56.670
of going after violent
extremist organizations,

00:10:56.670 --> 00:10:59.040
but is probably gonna have
a higher priority of trying

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:00.900
to posture to be effective

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:03.210
and to contribute to our success

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:06.300
in high intensity major theater war.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:08.430
So I think these are some of the things

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:10.110
that we need to think about

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:11.760
as we look into the near term,

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:13.200
into the midterm.

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:15.090
I'd mentioned one other point in passing,

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:16.620
which I do think is important

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:18.990
and which I would call your attention to,

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:21.750
which is when you have an explosion

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:24.450
of SOF the way that we had
in the last 20 years or so,

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:26.700
it can only come at the expense

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:30.270
of the leadership cadre
of our conventional units.

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:33.960
So by skimming the cream
off the conventional units,

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:35.460
there is a cost.

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:37.920
I submit to you pretty high cost.

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:39.360
Army special forces

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:42.690
or the Army's SOF
community probably counts

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:46.170
for something on the order of
5,000 non-commission officers,

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:49.980
many of whom are at that
critical E-6 and E-7 level

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:52.200
that are deposited now
in the SOF community

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:54.720
and can't be leading soldiers
in the conventional force.

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:56.340
That's a really big number.

00:11:56.340 --> 00:12:00.870
That's enough NCOs really to
cadre about an Army Corps.

00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:03.240
So I guess the relevant question here is,

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:06.180
is the return on that investment better

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:08.970
by putting them in the SOF community?

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:11.340
Or would the return on
that investment be better

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:14.190
by leading soldiers in the
conventional community?

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:16.320
I think that's an interesting question.

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:17.190
A quick vignette,

00:12:17.190 --> 00:12:18.570
my son was a platoon leader

00:12:18.570 --> 00:12:20.430
in the 82nd Airborne Division the day

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:22.650
that Qasem Soleimani was killed.

00:12:22.650 --> 00:12:24.450
His battalion was on DEER F1.

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:29.380
So no notice they blew out
to Iraq to secure the embassy

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:33.150
with none of its E-6
squad leaders authorized.

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:35.490
They had all departed for the Q Course.

00:12:35.490 --> 00:12:37.710
And he was the fourth platoon
leader in that platoon

00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:39.690
in one calendar year.

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:41.700
And his previous three
lieutenants had all departed

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:44.040
for special forces or
their Ranger Regiment.

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:46.050
And this is in the 82nd Airborne Division

00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:48.210
which, in theory, is
supposed to be maintained

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:51.510
at the highest level of manning
and readiness that we have.

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:54.630
So it's a tough problem to deal with.

00:12:54.630 --> 00:12:57.570
As a battalion commander in the 82nd,

00:12:57.570 --> 00:13:02.570
I lost 16 NCOs in one
calendar year to the Q Course.

00:13:02.670 --> 00:13:03.963
That's a crippling blow.

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:06.180
And you don't really have a vote

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:08.490
as a commander about
when these folks leave,

00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:10.290
when it's time for them
to go to the selection,

00:13:10.290 --> 00:13:11.190
they go to selection.

00:13:11.190 --> 00:13:12.960
And you don't really get
a vote in all of that.

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:16.981
So I'll leave it to others to say

00:13:16.981 --> 00:13:18.900
if a price worth paying,

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:20.250
I just want a flag for you

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:23.130
that there is a cost or a price to be paid

00:13:23.130 --> 00:13:25.770
in terms of pulling a
lot of quality leaders

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:27.750
out of the conventional force

00:13:27.750 --> 00:13:30.510
as we repost for great power competition

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:33.660
and we move away from sort
of the COIN G-1 environment.

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:34.493
I'll close for that,

00:13:34.493 --> 00:13:35.850
but look forward to your questions.

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:36.683
I hope there'll be some.

00:13:36.683 --> 00:13:38.130
Thanks very much.

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:40.200
- [Peter] My guess is there might be.

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:41.033
Colonel Jewell,

00:13:41.033 --> 00:13:45.030
since you are one of
the employees of SOCOM

00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:46.533
as part of JSOU,

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:50.093
over to you.

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:51.803
- Thank you.

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:54.210
Thank you, Richard.

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:55.310
- You're very welcome.

00:13:56.349 --> 00:13:57.914
- There's a lot in that.

00:13:57.914 --> 00:14:00.120
And I'll address a little bit of it

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:01.590
after I give you a little pitch

00:14:01.590 --> 00:14:02.423
from our perspective

00:14:02.423 --> 00:14:05.670
at the Joint Special Operations University

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:10.440
and the center that I am the
director of has a long name,

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:11.640
and Peter got it right.

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:14.520
But we basically run the
long term research projects

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:16.500
for the university.

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:18.867
This goes to something
Secretary Logan said

00:14:18.867 --> 00:14:20.190
and something you said, Richard,

00:14:20.190 --> 00:14:24.570
about learning and how
to adapt to what we did.

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:26.310
So our long-term history projects are

00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:28.263
social science research,

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:32.160
history in theory informed,

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:36.270
applied to current
problems in the command,

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:39.180
in the enterprise specifically.

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:41.220
We're aligned now with PACOM,

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:42.360
or sorry, SOCPAC,

00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:44.517
and SOC-Q , SOC-F,

00:14:44.517 --> 00:14:48.030
and SOCSOUTH on four of our key products.

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:50.580
One is serving directly to our commander

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:51.870
and his command teams

00:14:51.870 --> 00:14:54.423
as they're looking at
how to transform SOCOM.

00:14:55.380 --> 00:14:58.560
People when transform as the
mantra in the headquarters,

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:01.620
how to transform Special
Operations command

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:03.180
and the SOF enterprise

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:05.790
to contribute SOF-peculiar capabilities

00:15:05.790 --> 00:15:08.220
to integrated deterrents

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:13.220
and compound security
competition to deter left of,

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:16.920
for example, China insists,

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:19.050
China invading Taiwan.

00:15:19.050 --> 00:15:19.883
For example,

00:15:19.883 --> 00:15:23.130
something changing in
Europe with our acute threat

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:26.490
even more troubling than it has occurred.

00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:28.470
And how do we build the force?

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:30.540
And yes, build is what we're
talking about, Richard.

00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:33.300
So that's a little opposite

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:35.790
of what your suggestions are there

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:38.730
to be able to contribute
those SOF-peculiar things

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:41.340
to integrated deterrents

00:15:41.340 --> 00:15:43.530
with joint interagency, intergovernmental,

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:46.293
multinational and commercial partners.

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:50.580
And these research projects are the engine

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:52.470
of what President Wilson,

00:15:52.470 --> 00:15:53.420
the JSOU president,

00:15:54.266 --> 00:15:56.220
calls SOCOM's think do tank

00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:57.930
because we don't do that

00:15:57.930 --> 00:16:00.000
just to serve those
commands we're working with,

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.100
we do that to cycle education

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:03.690
and create knowledge back

00:16:03.690 --> 00:16:04.710
into the education

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:08.523
of the combined joint SOF
professionals for life.

00:16:10.230 --> 00:16:11.730
That's a sales pitch.

00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:12.780
I'm still practicing it.

00:16:12.780 --> 00:16:14.763
I've been in the job since January 1st.

00:16:16.050 --> 00:16:17.190
But during that time,

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:20.430
we sat in on the SOCOM's
commander's decision

00:16:20.430 --> 00:16:21.570
round tables,

00:16:21.570 --> 00:16:22.646
looking at a journey

00:16:22.646 --> 00:16:26.940
as he describes it to transform SOF,

00:16:26.940 --> 00:16:30.540
transform both the way
we employ the forces,

00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:33.910
pushing that more down to
the TSOCs and the commands

00:16:35.190 --> 00:16:39.723
and how we manage the force as
a service-like organization.

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:46.110
That's a pretty big step
after the GWOT years.

00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:50.697
One of the theories that
JSOU proposes is a history

00:16:50.697 --> 00:16:53.520
and theory-based background
to look at this practice.

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:55.293
It's called the Four Ages of SOF.

00:16:56.190 --> 00:17:00.990
I think Ben's book
doesn't use those terms,

00:17:00.990 --> 00:17:03.630
but he landed on very
similar things in it.

00:17:03.630 --> 00:17:05.070
So in the modern era of SOF,

00:17:05.070 --> 00:17:07.080
not counting Special Operations,

00:17:07.080 --> 00:17:08.130
unconventional warfare,

00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:11.070
regular warfare that occurred
long before our civil war,

00:17:11.070 --> 00:17:13.230
that occurred long before our revolution

00:17:13.230 --> 00:17:16.413
that are worth studying in
places as that history in theory.

00:17:17.850 --> 00:17:20.070
But we started about
the time of World War II

00:17:20.070 --> 00:17:21.390
when we started bringing together SOF

00:17:21.390 --> 00:17:24.120
as formations to apply in that context,

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:25.560
support conventional operations.

00:17:25.560 --> 00:17:29.370
We weren't really doing
a lot before, a lot.

00:17:29.370 --> 00:17:32.010
We were doing some with our
partners for British partners.

00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:34.470
Then in the '60s in the Vietnam era,

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:35.670
we grew the force

00:17:35.670 --> 00:17:38.970
to go along with more or
less conventional operations,

00:17:38.970 --> 00:17:40.350
relatively low intensity,

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:42.000
which is a term I don't love,

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:45.210
but still the war in Vietnam.

00:17:45.210 --> 00:17:48.810
And then after operation Eagle Claw was

00:17:48.810 --> 00:17:51.150
a huge threshold moment
that we didn't foresee.

00:17:51.150 --> 00:17:52.950
One of those tragedies,

00:17:52.950 --> 00:17:54.210
one of those great tragedies.

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:58.080
Our conference room in JSOU
is named after Eagle Claw.

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:00.450
The changes that came
from law, from Congress,

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:03.330
not from the command that set in motion,

00:18:03.330 --> 00:18:05.190
the creation of Special Operations command

00:18:05.190 --> 00:18:08.190
that set in motion the creation
of the assistant secretary,

00:18:08.190 --> 00:18:12.270
director of SO/LIC to go
forward in SOF-peculiar things.

00:18:12.270 --> 00:18:13.863
It also included education.

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:16.560
Mr. Locker,

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:18.330
who is one of the champions of that,

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:20.160
who we talk with every now and then,

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:21.291
as I said,

00:18:21.291 --> 00:18:22.140
that they didn't get the
education part right.

00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:23.840
They did say we have a university,

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:27.150
but we didn't get to all
the things we think we need

00:18:27.150 --> 00:18:29.823
to educate SOF professionals for life.

00:18:30.660 --> 00:18:32.220
And some people, Dave's mind,

00:18:32.220 --> 00:18:34.860
I think we are leaning towards
being more service-like.

00:18:34.860 --> 00:18:37.110
That's Dave's opinion.

00:18:37.110 --> 00:18:39.780
How much more secretary?

00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:43.170
A lot of the people have
clearer visions on it.,

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:44.310
but that goes kind of counter

00:18:44.310 --> 00:18:46.320
to what Richard was saying here.

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:48.870
I grew up in the conventional
army almost entirely

00:18:48.870 --> 00:18:50.127
in the intelligence community.

00:18:50.127 --> 00:18:52.517
And I watched a lot of
great talent move over

00:18:52.517 --> 00:18:55.290
in the Special Operations community.

00:18:55.290 --> 00:18:56.460
I just took that as a challenge

00:18:56.460 --> 00:18:58.560
to develop the talent we have

00:18:58.560 --> 00:19:01.290
and keep them enlisted to
what their core tasks are,

00:19:01.290 --> 00:19:03.390
where they're different from SOF-peculiar.

00:19:04.590 --> 00:19:06.720
So now we have another threshold moment.

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:07.740
Again, Eagle Claw,

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:09.450
9/11 was another one

00:19:09.450 --> 00:19:11.250
that caused us to go predominantly

00:19:11.250 --> 00:19:13.650
towards the CT functions

00:19:13.650 --> 00:19:15.570
and towards crisis response,

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:18.370
which remains our no fail function in SOF

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:20.093
at the expense

00:19:20.093 --> 00:19:23.010
of some of the other
other 12 functions we did.

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:24.780
And now at this threshold,

00:19:24.780 --> 00:19:27.270
after the end of the GWOT wars,

00:19:27.270 --> 00:19:30.180
we think to support integrated deterrents

00:19:30.180 --> 00:19:33.240
to support integrated
deterrents in competition.

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:37.200
The pasture remains fertile

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:41.793
to grow in the areas of civil
affairs for internal defense,

00:19:42.810 --> 00:19:44.700
countering weapons of mass destruction,

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:46.950
which SOF just actually
got the responsibility for

00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:48.090
as recently as 2016,

00:19:48.090 --> 00:19:50.910
even though it had been
one of our core activities

00:19:50.910 --> 00:19:52.310
going back to the beginning.

00:19:53.310 --> 00:19:54.780
And at JSOU,

00:19:54.780 --> 00:19:57.840
we're trying to set the
education for SOF professionals

00:19:57.840 --> 00:20:00.450
so that we can learn from the bad examples

00:20:00.450 --> 00:20:01.380
and the good examples,

00:20:01.380 --> 00:20:02.880
apply theory to it,

00:20:02.880 --> 00:20:04.170
social science theory,

00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:06.360
international relations theory,

00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:08.160
apply technology,

00:20:08.160 --> 00:20:10.680
technology to enable the way we learn.

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:13.170
In the conversation earlier
about machine learning

00:20:13.170 --> 00:20:14.910
and artificial intelligence.

00:20:14.910 --> 00:20:15.877
I was interested and I said,

00:20:15.877 --> 00:20:17.723
"I got some things to say,

00:20:17.723 --> 00:20:19.950
but that's not my expertise."

00:20:19.950 --> 00:20:22.380
But one thing we're
doing with some of that

00:20:22.380 --> 00:20:26.010
in JSAU is trying to empower our education

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:30.900
with geospatial tools informed
by current social media,

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:34.620
current news and classical theory,

00:20:34.620 --> 00:20:38.220
geospatially accessible
at the unclassified level

00:20:38.220 --> 00:20:42.540
for our professor, for
our students, for units.

00:20:42.540 --> 00:20:44.850
Much like the old CIA handbooks,

00:20:44.850 --> 00:20:46.410
when I first went to a foreign country

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:48.063
in the early '90s,

00:20:49.080 --> 00:20:50.490
make one of those that's enriched

00:20:50.490 --> 00:20:52.620
with current geospatial data.

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:54.000
That's part of one of our research.

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:56.670
It's alongside our research projects

00:20:56.670 --> 00:20:59.400
because that information
is very difficult to find

00:20:59.400 --> 00:21:00.990
in a unified space,

00:21:00.990 --> 00:21:02.820
certainly on the unclassified level,

00:21:02.820 --> 00:21:04.713
which is where we're working.

00:21:06.990 --> 00:21:09.040
And there is one thing I wanted to get to

00:21:10.081 --> 00:21:11.220
to kind of respond to Richard.

00:21:11.220 --> 00:21:12.120
It's not a debate.

00:21:14.400 --> 00:21:15.233
But again,

00:21:15.233 --> 00:21:16.066
I'm a little army intel guy,

00:21:16.066 --> 00:21:17.070
and I'm used to the three telling me

00:21:17.070 --> 00:21:17.940
I'm wrong about the enemy,

00:21:17.940 --> 00:21:19.973
and I'll say I've studied it for a while.

00:21:22.650 --> 00:21:24.750
One, I don't know the answer to why

00:21:24.750 --> 00:21:26.430
somebody reported the whole SOF formation

00:21:26.430 --> 00:21:28.170
as the same as the headquartes.

00:21:28.170 --> 00:21:29.460
I don't know.

00:21:29.460 --> 00:21:30.960
that's not correct.

00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:32.710
The other number was about correct.

00:21:34.320 --> 00:21:35.153
and the money-

00:21:35.153 --> 00:21:37.310
- It was the Secretary of
Defense who reported it.

00:21:37.310 --> 00:21:38.760
- Okay.

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:39.760
- Not just somebody.

00:21:40.680 --> 00:21:41.700
- Out of my realm

00:21:41.700 --> 00:21:43.710
and I don't doubt the report.

00:21:43.710 --> 00:21:45.813
We don't have 73,000 people in Tampa.

00:21:47.010 --> 00:21:47.843
- I'm sure you don't.

00:21:47.843 --> 00:21:48.676
- We do not.

00:21:50.370 --> 00:21:51.840
They wouldn't even all fit
in the football stadium

00:21:51.840 --> 00:21:53.273
where the bugs are right now.

00:21:55.317 --> 00:21:56.790
And the money, again,

00:21:56.790 --> 00:21:57.743
Mr. Nessen, and the AT&amp;L,

00:21:57.743 --> 00:22:00.000
and the technology world outside of me,

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:02.850
but the SOF-peculiar of some

00:22:02.850 --> 00:22:07.850
of the weapon systems you
named is a different addition

00:22:07.980 --> 00:22:08.813
to the force.

00:22:08.813 --> 00:22:11.670
It's a different addition to deterrence,

00:22:11.670 --> 00:22:15.423
integrated deterrence
to influence, I think.

00:22:16.470 --> 00:22:17.910
I've asked myself in the times,

00:22:17.910 --> 00:22:22.910
why don't we have the RMC130
and the conventional force?

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:24.983
I don't know.

00:22:24.983 --> 00:22:27.750
But they do provide something
in a way of low visibility,

00:22:27.750 --> 00:22:32.750
something in a way of unique
capacity that is SOF-peculiar.

00:22:34.050 --> 00:22:35.850
And if SOCOM

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:38.250
and specifically if JSOU is educating

00:22:38.250 --> 00:22:42.390
on something that is
not mostly SOF-peculiar,

00:22:42.390 --> 00:22:45.540
then we're stepping on the
grounds of the services

00:22:45.540 --> 00:22:46.373
or other folks.

00:22:46.373 --> 00:22:48.930
And so we try to find what SOF-peculiar,

00:22:48.930 --> 00:22:51.270
what's combined joint SOF-peculiar

00:22:51.270 --> 00:22:53.730
in a GMC joint,
interagency, international,

00:22:53.730 --> 00:22:54.813
or intergovernmental.

00:22:55.680 --> 00:22:56.513
Darn it,

00:22:56.513 --> 00:22:57.840
I always forget it.

00:22:57.840 --> 00:23:00.930
The multinational and
commercial environment.

00:23:00.930 --> 00:23:02.900
And those are partnerships that JSOU

00:23:02.900 --> 00:23:05.673
as part of SOCOM has where we are,

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:08.333
as an organization,

00:23:08.333 --> 00:23:11.073
unencumbered by the classified
work that SOCOM does.

00:23:12.270 --> 00:23:15.000
President Wilson went
to Taiwan in December

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:16.703
to have a discussion

00:23:16.703 --> 00:23:18.150
with the Taiwan National
Defense University,

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:19.830
that's not the exact name of it,

00:23:19.830 --> 00:23:21.063
education-based,

00:23:21.960 --> 00:23:24.630
which hadn't been done at that level.

00:23:24.630 --> 00:23:28.030
That included tens of two and three star

00:23:29.160 --> 00:23:31.432
Republic of China, military,

00:23:31.432 --> 00:23:33.810
and Department of Foreign Affairs leaders,

00:23:33.810 --> 00:23:36.900
talking about what they
educationally think

00:23:36.900 --> 00:23:39.510
they need to be concerned
about the security of Taiwan.

00:23:39.510 --> 00:23:40.863
Small contribution,

00:23:42.030 --> 00:23:43.890
building a relationship

00:23:43.890 --> 00:23:46.290
that is not to replace

00:23:46.290 --> 00:23:48.480
what the military formations are doing.

00:23:48.480 --> 00:23:50.640
But then we come back and talk about that.

00:23:50.640 --> 00:23:51.473
In fact,

00:23:51.473 --> 00:23:53.567
INDOPACOM had people
there and so did SOCPAC.

00:23:55.170 --> 00:23:57.990
So that's part of what we're
trying to do to work between.

00:23:57.990 --> 00:23:59.433
So I'm an employee at SOCOM.

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:02.550
I'm the guy that my president
looks at most of the time

00:24:02.550 --> 00:24:04.170
to work with what our headquarters does

00:24:04.170 --> 00:24:05.490
because before I came to this job,

00:24:05.490 --> 00:24:08.790
I was in our headquarters
being a typical normal military

00:24:08.790 --> 00:24:09.623
planner guy.

00:24:11.220 --> 00:24:13.710
and try to translate
that back into research

00:24:13.710 --> 00:24:16.410
for the education of SOF
professionals for life.

00:24:16.410 --> 00:24:18.030
I think I've gone too long.

00:24:18.030 --> 00:24:18.863
And I will stop.

00:24:18.863 --> 00:24:19.696
- Thank you.

00:24:19.696 --> 00:24:20.529
Thank you.

00:24:20.529 --> 00:24:22.546
I'm gonna turn to Colonel Collins

00:24:22.546 --> 00:24:25.140
who's also a retired
Special Forces officer.

00:24:25.140 --> 00:24:28.718
I just want to inject also
just into the conversation

00:24:28.718 --> 00:24:31.200
as we move forward.

00:24:31.200 --> 00:24:33.630
One of the premises of this discussion,

00:24:33.630 --> 00:24:37.560
I think maybe we should
try and unpack a bit,

00:24:37.560 --> 00:24:40.050
which is the GWOT is over,

00:24:40.050 --> 00:24:43.170
and it's all about
great power competition.

00:24:43.170 --> 00:24:44.550
And I'm reminded,

00:24:44.550 --> 00:24:47.100
and you can tell me if I'm
wrong, Colonel Collins,

00:24:47.100 --> 00:24:48.480
but at West Point,

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:49.313
the generation,

00:24:49.313 --> 00:24:52.350
the McChrystal betrays Odierno generation,

00:24:52.350 --> 00:24:54.930
the Vietnam War was almost
like a verboten subject,

00:24:54.930 --> 00:24:57.750
and it was sort of put
in the rear view mirror.

00:24:57.750 --> 00:25:01.500
And are we making some version
of the same mistake now?

00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:03.540
Because obviously the
National Security Defense...

00:25:03.540 --> 00:25:06.780
Well, the Trump National
Security Defense strategy,

00:25:06.780 --> 00:25:08.520
the Biden, they're very similar.

00:25:08.520 --> 00:25:09.990
They emphasize the same things.

00:25:09.990 --> 00:25:14.370
But are we gonna make another
version of the same mistake,

00:25:14.370 --> 00:25:16.860
which is by putting this
thing in the rear view mirror,

00:25:16.860 --> 00:25:19.440
basically unprepared for
the next time something

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:20.583
like this comes up?

00:25:22.080 --> 00:25:23.700
- Yeah, I mean I think
there's less of mistake

00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:24.870
of that happening this time,

00:25:24.870 --> 00:25:26.580
but I mean the DoD,

00:25:26.580 --> 00:25:27.840
we love to swing that pendulum.

00:25:27.840 --> 00:25:30.030
And if you wanna justify
a $700 billion budget,

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:32.370
you have to have a huge
enemy to justify that budget.

00:25:32.370 --> 00:25:35.550
So you gotta have an enemy out there

00:25:35.550 --> 00:25:37.650
to justify a massive budget.

00:25:37.650 --> 00:25:38.820
So you have that.

00:25:38.820 --> 00:25:39.653
And so, yeah,

00:25:39.653 --> 00:25:42.200
talking about the swing to
the great power competition.

00:25:43.320 --> 00:25:44.160
I think a few years ago,

00:25:44.160 --> 00:25:46.680
SOCOM at times has kind of
had this identity crisis

00:25:46.680 --> 00:25:47.734
of what's our role gonna be in this?

00:25:47.734 --> 00:25:49.560
Are we gonna lose our budget shares?

00:25:49.560 --> 00:25:51.360
We kind of switch to
great power competition,

00:25:51.360 --> 00:25:52.740
which is kind of unfathomable to me

00:25:52.740 --> 00:25:54.570
because you just have
to look at our history

00:25:54.570 --> 00:25:55.890
or look at how they're employed,

00:25:55.890 --> 00:25:57.720
and that's not a fear.

00:25:57.720 --> 00:25:58.740
But I think there's a few reasons

00:25:58.740 --> 00:26:01.800
why this fear was
probably why they had it.

00:26:01.800 --> 00:26:02.820
I mean one is I've argued

00:26:02.820 --> 00:26:04.110
with Mike Sheehan and Mark Mitchell

00:26:04.110 --> 00:26:06.750
about why the office is called ASD SO/LIC

00:26:06.750 --> 00:26:09.600
that get rid of low intensity
conflict from the title.

00:26:09.600 --> 00:26:11.520
Names and titles matter,

00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:12.750
means the rest of the services.

00:26:12.750 --> 00:26:14.580
Anything that deal with
low intensity conflict,

00:26:14.580 --> 00:26:15.840
they just push off to SOF

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:17.580
because that's where it belongs.

00:26:17.580 --> 00:26:20.550
It belongs in this office that's not ours

00:26:20.550 --> 00:26:21.990
when they have a role in it.

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:23.970
And then SOF is then also left wondering,

00:26:23.970 --> 00:26:27.180
if we belong to Special
Operations low intensity conflict,

00:26:27.180 --> 00:26:29.010
what's our role in
great power competition?

00:26:29.010 --> 00:26:30.810
Because that maybe isn't our realm

00:26:30.810 --> 00:26:31.770
when it's always been the realm.

00:26:31.770 --> 00:26:33.180
So just fix the title of the office

00:26:33.180 --> 00:26:34.740
and you'll fix some of these problems.

00:26:34.740 --> 00:26:36.150
And the third thing is we all know,

00:26:36.150 --> 00:26:38.280
it's not low intensity if
you're the one getting shot at.

00:26:38.280 --> 00:26:40.920
That's pretty fricking high
intense when that's going on.

00:26:40.920 --> 00:26:42.120
But I mean, titles matter

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:44.070
and I think that's one thing.

00:26:44.070 --> 00:26:46.140
But in terms of the
great power competition,

00:26:46.140 --> 00:26:47.370
I've always hated this name, too.

00:26:47.370 --> 00:26:48.723
It's great power conflict.

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:51.810
If you think we're not at
conflict with Russia and China,

00:26:51.810 --> 00:26:52.643
you're wrong.

00:26:52.643 --> 00:26:53.550
Ask them.

00:26:53.550 --> 00:26:55.230
Ask Chinese and ask the Russians.

00:26:55.230 --> 00:26:56.640
We are at conflict, right?

00:26:56.640 --> 00:26:59.580
The problem is we have this
very narrow binary definition

00:26:59.580 --> 00:27:01.290
of war and not war

00:27:01.290 --> 00:27:03.390
and that's not how they view the world.

00:27:03.390 --> 00:27:05.040
And when we view it from that perspective,

00:27:05.040 --> 00:27:07.050
we're always gonna be a disadvantage

00:27:07.050 --> 00:27:08.970
for this great power competition.

00:27:08.970 --> 00:27:10.143
So I hate that word.

00:27:11.010 --> 00:27:11.843
But again,

00:27:11.843 --> 00:27:12.955
it's unfathomable

00:27:12.955 --> 00:27:13.980
to me that like SOF
would sometimes question

00:27:13.980 --> 00:27:15.870
what our role is in this.

00:27:15.870 --> 00:27:16.980
When we've always had a role,

00:27:16.980 --> 00:27:19.200
we're gonna be employed, right?

00:27:19.200 --> 00:27:20.640
As Erin said earlier,

00:27:20.640 --> 00:27:21.690
massive rule in this.

00:27:21.690 --> 00:27:24.210
But I think part of the
problem is the coming out

00:27:24.210 --> 00:27:25.620
of the GWOT in 20 years of this,

00:27:25.620 --> 00:27:26.640
so that's 20 years.

00:27:26.640 --> 00:27:28.290
So you have an entire
generation of officers.

00:27:28.290 --> 00:27:30.360
That's all they know, right?

00:27:30.360 --> 00:27:31.440
And during this,

00:27:31.440 --> 00:27:33.570
I will agree with some of
the points Richard made

00:27:33.570 --> 00:27:35.370
and I'll disagree with others,

00:27:35.370 --> 00:27:38.040
but part of it was there
was way too much SOF doing

00:27:38.040 --> 00:27:40.470
direct action during the conflict, right?

00:27:40.470 --> 00:27:42.060
Maybe the COs should be doing it,

00:27:42.060 --> 00:27:43.530
Rangers should be doing it,

00:27:43.530 --> 00:27:45.150
select elements of SOF doing it.

00:27:45.150 --> 00:27:46.170
But too many times,

00:27:46.170 --> 00:27:48.630
special forces elements
were doing direct action

00:27:48.630 --> 00:27:50.640
when they should be building
a host nation force.

00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:51.840
And that was not helpful.

00:27:51.840 --> 00:27:54.420
I remember (indistinct) in 2004,

00:27:54.420 --> 00:27:55.770
talking to ODB commanders,

00:27:55.770 --> 00:27:59.850
so an SF company commander that
had six detachments in Iraq.

00:27:59.850 --> 00:28:02.130
And he was being directed to
do foreign internal defense.

00:28:02.130 --> 00:28:03.450
And he said, "No, I don't wanna do that.

00:28:03.450 --> 00:28:04.590
We wanna go do targets."

00:28:04.590 --> 00:28:08.700
I'm like, "As opposed to
developing a 350-man battalion

00:28:08.700 --> 00:28:10.950
and then you'd have
all these intel assets,

00:28:10.950 --> 00:28:12.180
for them to hit targets,

00:28:12.180 --> 00:28:14.520
they could hit so many
more than you could hit."

00:28:14.520 --> 00:28:16.950
But part of the problem is
that's what SOF attracts.

00:28:16.950 --> 00:28:18.000
SOF attracts a bunch of people

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:19.050
that wanna do direct action

00:28:19.050 --> 00:28:21.213
and that is not the primary role of SOF.

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:24.180
for a small number of SOF forces,

00:28:24.180 --> 00:28:25.013
that's it.

00:28:25.013 --> 00:28:27.120
You're talking National
Mission Force, Rangers,

00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:27.953
and the Army.

00:28:27.953 --> 00:28:28.786
That's the main thing

00:28:28.786 --> 00:28:29.730
not special forces,

00:28:29.730 --> 00:28:31.620
but that's who they attract.

00:28:31.620 --> 00:28:33.690
And then you wonder why
you have this dissonance

00:28:33.690 --> 00:28:35.190
between what they want to do

00:28:35.190 --> 00:28:36.490
and what they're gonna do.

00:28:38.250 --> 00:28:40.850
To hit up on some of the
thing Richard talked about.

00:28:41.910 --> 00:28:43.530
I'm not sure.

00:28:43.530 --> 00:28:44.730
I don't doubt that was the case

00:28:44.730 --> 00:28:47.340
in his environment about the black SOF

00:28:47.340 --> 00:28:48.390
or white SOF running around

00:28:48.390 --> 00:28:49.380
and not being coordinated.

00:28:49.380 --> 00:28:51.300
I can just tell you everywhere I operated,

00:28:51.300 --> 00:28:52.680
that was never a problem.

00:28:52.680 --> 00:28:53.790
Before I did any raid,

00:28:53.790 --> 00:28:56.160
I talked to the owning force
on the ground about it.

00:28:56.160 --> 00:28:57.000
After we got done,

00:28:57.000 --> 00:28:57.850
I talked to them.

00:28:58.920 --> 00:29:00.960
And that was whether it
was with General Odierno

00:29:00.960 --> 00:29:03.480
on down to lower level or General Mattis.

00:29:03.480 --> 00:29:04.420
I remember a time

00:29:05.430 --> 00:29:08.910
when General McChrystal's
building all the liaison networks,

00:29:08.910 --> 00:29:09.960
expending them everywhere,

00:29:09.960 --> 00:29:12.127
and he came to visit
General Mattis and he says,

00:29:12.127 --> 00:29:13.530
"I don't need a liaison officer,

00:29:13.530 --> 00:29:15.330
I got Liam here."

00:29:15.330 --> 00:29:16.163
But he was right,

00:29:16.163 --> 00:29:17.190
he needed a liaison officer

00:29:17.190 --> 00:29:18.840
'cause I'm working full-time.

00:29:18.840 --> 00:29:20.940
I can't spend all my time at the division,

00:29:21.960 --> 00:29:23.070
but I disagree with that.

00:29:23.070 --> 00:29:25.350
We had unity of effort
everywhere I was at,

00:29:25.350 --> 00:29:27.870
even if we didn't have unity of command.

00:29:27.870 --> 00:29:28.710
They're gonna be different

00:29:28.710 --> 00:29:30.120
and that's the problem.

00:29:30.120 --> 00:29:31.020
that's gotta be addressed,

00:29:31.020 --> 00:29:33.390
but I don't think that's the solution.

00:29:33.390 --> 00:29:35.190
And the owning forces,

00:29:35.190 --> 00:29:36.120
it was never a problem.

00:29:36.120 --> 00:29:36.953
I remember another,

00:29:36.953 --> 00:29:37.830
Fourth Infantry Division,

00:29:37.830 --> 00:29:42.780
they had a brigade operations
officer called me up one time

00:29:42.780 --> 00:29:44.340
and said, "Hey, do you know
anything about this target?

00:29:44.340 --> 00:29:46.170
It was white SEALs who
were hitting the target,"

00:29:46.170 --> 00:29:47.700
and I said, "Nope.

00:29:47.700 --> 00:29:48.901
Just tell them not.

00:29:48.901 --> 00:29:49.734
Did they tell you why they're hitting it?

00:29:49.734 --> 00:29:50.567
Or who it is?"

00:29:50.567 --> 00:29:51.400
And they said no.

00:29:51.400 --> 00:29:52.320
I said, "Tell them they can't hit it.

00:29:52.320 --> 00:29:53.640
You own the terrain.

00:29:53.640 --> 00:29:55.860
They can't hit it if you
tell them not to hit it."

00:29:55.860 --> 00:29:58.530
I'm like, "I tell you every
single target I'm gonna hit

00:29:58.530 --> 00:29:59.363
because for all I know,

00:29:59.363 --> 00:30:00.196
you have a relationship with them,

00:30:00.196 --> 00:30:01.260
I don't even need to hit the target

00:30:01.260 --> 00:30:03.750
'cause you can go out
and get them for me."

00:30:03.750 --> 00:30:06.063
And so I disagree with
that to some extent.

00:30:08.340 --> 00:30:10.020
The talent problem of the talent drain

00:30:10.020 --> 00:30:11.190
that you were talking about, Richard,

00:30:11.190 --> 00:30:12.750
I mean I think that's not a problem

00:30:12.750 --> 00:30:13.830
of SOF stealing people.

00:30:13.830 --> 00:30:15.390
I think it's a problem
with the conventional army

00:30:15.390 --> 00:30:18.120
that people wouldn't be
leaving it more than likely

00:30:18.120 --> 00:30:20.430
in terms of why are they going to SOF

00:30:20.430 --> 00:30:22.290
because it's not being handled right.

00:30:22.290 --> 00:30:24.570
They aren't seeing satisfaction
in conventional army.

00:30:24.570 --> 00:30:28.290
And the Ranger Regiment actually
is a benefit to the army.

00:30:28.290 --> 00:30:29.580
They don't go there and stay there.

00:30:29.580 --> 00:30:31.050
They go out there to Ranger Regiment,

00:30:31.050 --> 00:30:33.670
and they come right back
after doing time there

00:30:34.950 --> 00:30:36.240
and take those skills.

00:30:36.240 --> 00:30:39.810
So I would disagree about
those forces stealing talent

00:30:39.810 --> 00:30:42.030
from the service.

00:30:42.030 --> 00:30:44.280
I think the problem's probably more

00:30:44.280 --> 00:30:47.643
with the service level than anything else.

00:30:52.260 --> 00:30:53.093
Yeah, I mean budget,

00:30:53.093 --> 00:30:54.030
yeah, I would agree right there.

00:30:54.030 --> 00:30:55.590
We have an insatiable,

00:30:55.590 --> 00:30:57.990
I mean amazing ability
to grow and spend money.

00:30:57.990 --> 00:30:59.550
I'm a little concerned not with SOF growth

00:30:59.550 --> 00:31:00.930
because there's plenty of missions there.

00:31:00.930 --> 00:31:02.460
We'll never have the missions that we...

00:31:02.460 --> 00:31:04.128
We'll always have more missions

00:31:04.128 --> 00:31:04.961
than we can ever execute again.

00:31:04.961 --> 00:31:06.390
Why should we ever have
an identity crisis?

00:31:06.390 --> 00:31:07.223
Never, right?

00:31:08.490 --> 00:31:10.740
There's more things we can
do than we will ever do

00:31:10.740 --> 00:31:12.540
and things that we should be doing.

00:31:12.540 --> 00:31:14.790
We're always gonna be short of people.

00:31:14.790 --> 00:31:17.670
My greatest fear with the
growth is we were never

00:31:17.670 --> 00:31:19.710
at a hundred percent before
we added 4th Battalions,

00:31:19.710 --> 00:31:20.820
we were never at a hundred percent

00:31:20.820 --> 00:31:22.320
before we added Squadrons.

00:31:22.320 --> 00:31:24.300
How the heck can you add,

00:31:24.300 --> 00:31:26.040
grow when you've never had...

00:31:26.040 --> 00:31:29.460
You just have so many talented
operators or analysts,

00:31:29.460 --> 00:31:31.200
or whatever it is to put in these places.

00:31:31.200 --> 00:31:33.570
That's the bigger problem to me.

00:31:33.570 --> 00:31:35.544
The people are the most important thing.

00:31:35.544 --> 00:31:36.377
And and I always joke,

00:31:36.377 --> 00:31:37.210
it wasn't a joke,

00:31:37.210 --> 00:31:40.140
but what's supposed to
be a 12-person detachment

00:31:40.140 --> 00:31:41.460
and you go from 9 to 10,

00:31:41.460 --> 00:31:44.130
but that 10th person is a negative.

00:31:44.130 --> 00:31:45.390
You've grown from nine to eight

00:31:45.390 --> 00:31:47.730
because you've gotta have
somebody watch this person

00:31:49.599 --> 00:31:53.460
that can cause massive
strategic harm to your nation

00:31:53.460 --> 00:31:55.080
by these people out there

00:31:55.080 --> 00:31:58.582
as I think Richard kind of
pointed out some of those things.

00:31:58.582 --> 00:32:01.170
The last thing I'll say in in terms of...

00:32:01.170 --> 00:32:02.820
We shouldn't insert.

00:32:02.820 --> 00:32:05.790
I'll talk about the forgotten
SOF forces here briefly&lt;

00:32:05.790 --> 00:32:08.760
civil affairs and PSYOPs
that we never talk about,

00:32:08.760 --> 00:32:10.160
but they're part of it, too.

00:32:11.040 --> 00:32:12.750
We should not send forces somewhere

00:32:12.750 --> 00:32:15.150
without actually having a way

00:32:15.150 --> 00:32:16.590
to actually measure their performance.

00:32:16.590 --> 00:32:17.423
And you can do it.

00:32:17.423 --> 00:32:19.950
Too often, we measure
measures of performance,

00:32:19.950 --> 00:32:21.570
not measures of effectiveness.

00:32:21.570 --> 00:32:22.920
And there's ways to do this, right?

00:32:22.920 --> 00:32:23.753
Survey data,

00:32:23.753 --> 00:32:24.930
other ways to do it.

00:32:24.930 --> 00:32:26.310
But was doing a project

00:32:26.310 --> 00:32:28.680
for one of the civil affairs groups

00:32:28.680 --> 00:32:31.770
'cause they had multiple
ailments out there in Europe,

00:32:31.770 --> 00:32:33.090
all doing kind of similar things

00:32:33.090 --> 00:32:34.500
for different purposes all over Europe.

00:32:34.500 --> 00:32:35.790
And I said, "Well, how do you
know if you're effective?"

00:32:35.790 --> 00:32:36.810
He's like, "I have no idea.

00:32:36.810 --> 00:32:38.400
That's why I'm coming to you

00:32:38.400 --> 00:32:40.170
and have the cadets look at this project.

00:32:40.170 --> 00:32:42.057
Are we actually being effective?"

00:32:42.990 --> 00:32:45.300
And so figuring that out, like,

00:32:45.300 --> 00:32:46.440
they're a small asset.

00:32:46.440 --> 00:32:49.050
Let's figure out where
we can employ this asset

00:32:49.050 --> 00:32:51.600
so we can get the most
return on the investment.

00:32:51.600 --> 00:32:53.067
And so you gotta think

00:32:53.067 --> 00:32:53.970
about how do you actually
measure that going in.

00:32:53.970 --> 00:32:55.350
And if we can't measure it,

00:32:55.350 --> 00:32:56.922
sometimes you...

00:32:56.922 --> 00:32:57.923
I mean there's ways to get at it.

00:32:59.460 --> 00:33:00.690
That should be part of the process.

00:33:00.690 --> 00:33:02.220
When you're thinking
about deploying an asset,

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:05.223
how do we measure its
actual effectiveness?

00:33:06.720 --> 00:33:08.550
What do we mean by effectiveness?

00:33:08.550 --> 00:33:10.230
So that's kind of it.

00:33:10.230 --> 00:33:11.063
But bottom line,

00:33:11.063 --> 00:33:12.330
I mean SOF has a major role to play

00:33:12.330 --> 00:33:13.920
in great power competition, right?

00:33:13.920 --> 00:33:16.020
Building host nation capability,

00:33:16.020 --> 00:33:18.030
access and placement,

00:33:18.030 --> 00:33:19.590
building these relationships we can lever.

00:33:19.590 --> 00:33:21.390
I mean, one of the best examples

00:33:21.390 --> 00:33:24.570
and a success story during
the last 20 years is OEFPI,

00:33:24.570 --> 00:33:25.680
the Philippines, right?

00:33:25.680 --> 00:33:26.910
Very small preference.

00:33:26.910 --> 00:33:27.743
Why did it work there?

00:33:27.743 --> 00:33:30.857
Because no one in the US
military cared about it

00:33:30.857 --> 00:33:32.910
'cause we were all in
Afghanistan and Iraq.

00:33:32.910 --> 00:33:35.610
So you had a very small force
being very effective there.

00:33:35.610 --> 00:33:37.050
And more often than not,

00:33:37.050 --> 00:33:38.190
SOF is most effective

00:33:38.190 --> 00:33:39.750
when we're actually forced to stay small,

00:33:39.750 --> 00:33:40.920
like we did in El Salvador,

00:33:40.920 --> 00:33:42.090
we did in Columbia.

00:33:42.090 --> 00:33:43.830
Again, Columbia wasn't
effective from counter drug,

00:33:43.830 --> 00:33:46.650
but effective from
counterinsurgency, right?

00:33:46.650 --> 00:33:47.483
Philippines, right?

00:33:47.483 --> 00:33:48.316
These are places.

00:33:48.316 --> 00:33:50.490
And so one of the lessons is,

00:33:50.490 --> 00:33:52.380
hey, we have to be artificially restricted

00:33:52.380 --> 00:33:54.150
to be in small or we will grow too big

00:33:54.150 --> 00:33:56.730
and you actually grow to be ineffective.

00:33:56.730 --> 00:33:57.780
Thanks, Peter.

00:33:57.780 --> 00:33:58.830
- Thank you, Ben.
- Oh what I forgot to mention,

00:33:58.830 --> 00:34:02.010
he didn't mention that I'm
also a fellow with New America,

00:34:02.010 --> 00:34:05.117
which Peter is the
president of New America.

00:34:05.117 --> 00:34:06.690
- [Peter] Vice president,

00:34:06.690 --> 00:34:07.653
but who cares?

00:34:08.820 --> 00:34:11.610
So Ben, I have a terrible pun here.

00:34:11.610 --> 00:34:13.020
You did the deepest of deep dives

00:34:13.020 --> 00:34:15.033
on the history of the US Navy SEALs.

00:34:16.380 --> 00:34:17.310
What are the lessons?

00:34:17.310 --> 00:34:18.270
I don't know,

00:34:18.270 --> 00:34:19.227
I'm only 150 pages in

00:34:19.227 --> 00:34:22.050
and I thought it was absolutely brilliant.

00:34:22.050 --> 00:34:23.460
One of the lessons I took away was

00:34:23.460 --> 00:34:26.100
how many times certain
units were kind of created

00:34:26.100 --> 00:34:27.990
for specific situations

00:34:27.990 --> 00:34:31.170
and then sort of disbanded over time

00:34:31.170 --> 00:34:34.470
as the rationale for their
existence seem to fade

00:34:34.470 --> 00:34:38.130
or because of bureaucratic in fighting.

00:34:38.130 --> 00:34:40.980
As you look forward to...

00:34:40.980 --> 00:34:43.410
The pivot, obviously, is from the GWOT

00:34:43.410 --> 00:34:44.670
to great power competition,

00:34:44.670 --> 00:34:46.590
even if you don't necessarily agree,

00:34:46.590 --> 00:34:48.840
that actually is the main...

00:34:48.840 --> 00:34:53.130
Yeah, that's where the
defense establishment is.

00:34:53.130 --> 00:34:56.460
Based on your sort of own
historical understanding

00:34:56.460 --> 00:34:57.293
of all this,

00:34:58.290 --> 00:35:01.080
how do you see this
playing out for the units

00:35:01.080 --> 00:35:05.403
that grew up so spectacularly
in the war against terrorism?

00:35:08.967 --> 00:35:10.530
- I don't know.

00:35:10.530 --> 00:35:11.363
- Great answer.

00:35:13.948 --> 00:35:14.781
- Make it up.

00:35:14.781 --> 00:35:15.614
- You're right, right.

00:35:15.614 --> 00:35:17.190
- Just make it up.

00:35:17.190 --> 00:35:18.023
- Thank you, Peter.

00:35:18.023 --> 00:35:19.426
Thanks for the question.

00:35:19.426 --> 00:35:20.259
Thanks for my co-panelists.

00:35:20.259 --> 00:35:21.540
And thanks for the invitation, Mike,

00:35:21.540 --> 00:35:22.373
to be here.

00:35:22.373 --> 00:35:23.206
This is my first panel

00:35:23.206 --> 00:35:24.483
so this is a treat.

00:35:27.000 --> 00:35:28.440
To catch everybody up

00:35:28.440 --> 00:35:30.900
to speed on what Peter is talking about.

00:35:30.900 --> 00:35:34.713
The early history of SOF
is in fact very choppy.

00:35:37.260 --> 00:35:39.453
There's periods of stagnation, explosion,

00:35:40.650 --> 00:35:42.870
merging of units together.

00:35:42.870 --> 00:35:43.950
One of the thing

00:35:43.950 --> 00:35:47.880
that Peter mentioned was
particularly the early creations

00:35:47.880 --> 00:35:49.770
and disbandment of various units.

00:35:49.770 --> 00:35:54.240
That happens lots of times.

00:35:54.240 --> 00:35:55.950
So probably the best examples

00:35:55.950 --> 00:35:57.510
of that are the Marine Corps Raiders

00:35:57.510 --> 00:35:59.430
that are created in 1942.

00:35:59.430 --> 00:36:02.160
They have some sort of
spectacular operations.

00:36:02.160 --> 00:36:04.887
They're publicized in a very
popular movie at the time.

00:36:04.887 --> 00:36:06.660
And a few weeks after the movie comes out,

00:36:06.660 --> 00:36:07.650
they're disbanded.

00:36:07.650 --> 00:36:09.450
Similar thing happens
with the Army Rangers.

00:36:09.450 --> 00:36:11.730
Army Rangers are created in 1942.

00:36:11.730 --> 00:36:13.230
Half of the Rangers are committed

00:36:13.230 --> 00:36:15.543
to a disastrous raid in Italy.

00:36:16.710 --> 00:36:18.750
They're disbanded.

00:36:18.750 --> 00:36:20.850
The three other Ranger
battalions continue on

00:36:20.850 --> 00:36:21.780
in World War II.

00:36:21.780 --> 00:36:23.400
1945, no more Rangers,

00:36:23.400 --> 00:36:25.200
Korean War comes along.

00:36:25.200 --> 00:36:28.860
Army needs a sort of a
rating capability again.

00:36:28.860 --> 00:36:30.300
They reestablished the Rangers.

00:36:30.300 --> 00:36:31.560
Eight months after that,

00:36:31.560 --> 00:36:33.570
Rangers are disbanded again.

00:36:33.570 --> 00:36:38.120
They're not reestablished as a real force.

00:36:39.060 --> 00:36:40.860
There's sort of Ranger type units

00:36:40.860 --> 00:36:43.770
that are brought back in Vietnam.

00:36:43.770 --> 00:36:44.603
But the Rangers,

00:36:44.603 --> 00:36:45.436
as we know them,

00:36:45.436 --> 00:36:47.970
are not really recreated until 1974.

00:36:47.970 --> 00:36:49.320
So there is this period

00:36:49.320 --> 00:36:54.320
of sort of creation,
disbandment, merging together.

00:36:54.540 --> 00:36:57.000
And so I think the question

00:36:57.000 --> 00:36:59.040
that we're all sort of
trying to understand

00:36:59.040 --> 00:37:00.930
in this room is,

00:37:00.930 --> 00:37:05.790
how do we move forward
into this new environment

00:37:05.790 --> 00:37:08.040
where we have these new threats,

00:37:08.040 --> 00:37:10.920
great power conflict as Liam said?

00:37:10.920 --> 00:37:13.350
Well, I think it's interesting to know,

00:37:13.350 --> 00:37:16.263
at least for that first
20, 30-year period of SOF,

00:37:17.490 --> 00:37:19.710
we were always in great power conflict.

00:37:19.710 --> 00:37:22.350
I mean, we've been in a new generation now

00:37:22.350 --> 00:37:27.350
where we've had this
maybe incorrect perception

00:37:27.900 --> 00:37:29.670
that we haven't been in
great power competition

00:37:29.670 --> 00:37:31.440
because our conflict,

00:37:31.440 --> 00:37:34.950
because we've been focusing
on insurgency groups,

00:37:34.950 --> 00:37:36.300
low intensity conflict stuff.

00:37:36.300 --> 00:37:38.100
But if we want to understand

00:37:38.100 --> 00:37:40.110
why we are in the current situation

00:37:40.110 --> 00:37:42.930
and how to project forward from that,

00:37:42.930 --> 00:37:45.600
how we have C-130s,

00:37:45.600 --> 00:37:50.600
why we have a colonel set
a potential talent drain

00:37:51.570 --> 00:37:53.610
from the regular service branches,

00:37:53.610 --> 00:37:58.610
why the ASD SO/LIC position
has that low intensity conflict

00:38:02.520 --> 00:38:04.200
acronym at the end of it,

00:38:04.200 --> 00:38:06.270
I think we have to
understand how we got here.

00:38:06.270 --> 00:38:07.740
And one trick that you learn is

00:38:07.740 --> 00:38:08.970
if you are a historian,

00:38:08.970 --> 00:38:10.470
if you're going to write history,

00:38:10.470 --> 00:38:12.630
is in order to understand why questions,

00:38:12.630 --> 00:38:14.413
the first thing that you need

00:38:14.413 --> 00:38:15.690
to do is understand what happened,

00:38:15.690 --> 00:38:16.860
put things in order.

00:38:16.860 --> 00:38:18.480
Why will always come to the surface

00:38:18.480 --> 00:38:20.530
if you just start to put things in order.

00:38:22.830 --> 00:38:26.550
And I think most of us have this idea

00:38:26.550 --> 00:38:30.663
that special operation history
is the story of tension,

00:38:31.800 --> 00:38:34.470
tension between conventional leaders

00:38:34.470 --> 00:38:36.003
and unconventional leaders.

00:38:38.040 --> 00:38:38.913
And that's true.

00:38:40.322 --> 00:38:42.570
It absolutely is true.

00:38:42.570 --> 00:38:45.450
There have been biases in the military

00:38:45.450 --> 00:38:48.900
against elite troops
from the very beginning

00:38:48.900 --> 00:38:50.310
of our history.

00:38:50.310 --> 00:38:51.510
Most notably,

00:38:51.510 --> 00:38:53.760
we start to see them in World War II.

00:38:53.760 --> 00:38:55.563
You have leaders like Mark Clark,

00:38:56.640 --> 00:39:00.930
even leaders like Eisenhower
and George Marshall

00:39:00.930 --> 00:39:02.640
that are very, very reluctant

00:39:02.640 --> 00:39:05.583
to create elite troops within the ranks.

00:39:06.900 --> 00:39:08.520
One, because they don't want other troops

00:39:08.520 --> 00:39:13.520
to feel like their
service is less important

00:39:13.620 --> 00:39:14.670
than the elite troops,

00:39:14.670 --> 00:39:16.050
but also for practical reasons.

00:39:16.050 --> 00:39:18.790
They don't want the best
troops in their organizations

00:39:19.680 --> 00:39:22.700
to flee and join those other more famous

00:39:26.040 --> 00:39:28.260
or more whatever you wanna call it,

00:39:28.260 --> 00:39:29.730
those other units.

00:39:29.730 --> 00:39:31.200
So there's practicalities,

00:39:31.200 --> 00:39:32.670
and those are absolutely all true.

00:39:32.670 --> 00:39:35.100
Those all happen and
they're part of the story

00:39:35.100 --> 00:39:35.940
and how we got here,

00:39:35.940 --> 00:39:37.830
and they're part of that tension.

00:39:37.830 --> 00:39:40.410
But it's also worth us remembering

00:39:40.410 --> 00:39:42.840
that all of the unconventional units

00:39:42.840 --> 00:39:47.070
that we have today were all
started by conventional leaders.

00:39:47.070 --> 00:39:51.300
We have leaders throughout our history

00:39:51.300 --> 00:39:53.220
that never served a day in anything,

00:39:53.220 --> 00:39:55.920
you would even consider remotely special

00:39:55.920 --> 00:39:57.120
or unconventional.

00:39:57.120 --> 00:40:02.120
Emerald King, Kelly Turner,
John McGee, Bill Darby,

00:40:03.450 --> 00:40:04.383
all of these guys,

00:40:05.730 --> 00:40:07.743
they all came from conventional units.

00:40:08.940 --> 00:40:10.650
So I think it's important

00:40:10.650 --> 00:40:15.243
to really understand how
the real story happened.

00:40:16.230 --> 00:40:18.240
We got here,

00:40:18.240 --> 00:40:22.380
the tension that existed is, I think,

00:40:22.380 --> 00:40:26.793
really was a result of partly
great power competition.

00:40:27.930 --> 00:40:31.230
It was difficult in those early
years of Special Operations

00:40:31.230 --> 00:40:34.650
to sort of figure out
where there was a place

00:40:34.650 --> 00:40:37.320
for these sort of behind the lines Raiders

00:40:37.320 --> 00:40:39.300
or behind the lines whatever.

00:40:39.300 --> 00:40:41.610
When I was doing research for the book,

00:40:41.610 --> 00:40:45.450
I sort of created a
little matrix for myself

00:40:45.450 --> 00:40:47.900
to understand what a
Special Operations unit was.

00:40:49.560 --> 00:40:50.910
With all the acronyms of things

00:40:50.910 --> 00:40:52.680
that Special Operations Forces do,

00:40:52.680 --> 00:40:53.880
I boiled it down into the,

00:40:53.880 --> 00:40:55.920
I call the 3Rs of Special Operations:

00:40:55.920 --> 00:40:57.483
recon, recking, and rating.

00:40:58.680 --> 00:41:02.220
But in order to understand
what a special operation was,

00:41:02.220 --> 00:41:03.480
I started to think

00:41:03.480 --> 00:41:06.600
about like what defines
a special operation

00:41:06.600 --> 00:41:08.040
'cause you could have an elite unit

00:41:08.040 --> 00:41:08.873
or a special unit

00:41:08.873 --> 00:41:11.370
that can be doing a conventional
operation or vice versa.

00:41:11.370 --> 00:41:12.480
You can have a conventional unit

00:41:12.480 --> 00:41:14.100
that's doing a special operation.

00:41:14.100 --> 00:41:15.540
So what is a special operation?

00:41:15.540 --> 00:41:16.373
So I started to think,

00:41:16.373 --> 00:41:18.540
okay, well, it's usually
behind enemy lines.

00:41:18.540 --> 00:41:19.770
And if it's not behind enemy lines,

00:41:19.770 --> 00:41:22.170
it's at least beyond the
forward line of troop.

00:41:22.170 --> 00:41:23.340
So if you're going to have a unit

00:41:23.340 --> 00:41:24.180
that's going to be operating

00:41:24.180 --> 00:41:26.400
in the forward line of
troops or behind enemy lines,

00:41:26.400 --> 00:41:31.400
it's gonna have to move
at night at some point.

00:41:32.160 --> 00:41:33.720
It's gonna have to get to its target

00:41:33.720 --> 00:41:36.390
or wherever it's gonna
have to be done at night.

00:41:36.390 --> 00:41:38.130
In order for that unit to move at night,

00:41:38.130 --> 00:41:40.920
it's going to have to be small.

00:41:40.920 --> 00:41:43.380
For a unit to move at night
effectively and quietly,

00:41:43.380 --> 00:41:44.520
it's gotta be small.

00:41:44.520 --> 00:41:45.660
And because it's small,

00:41:45.660 --> 00:41:50.310
it has to be targeting a
target that's vulnerable.

00:41:50.310 --> 00:41:52.830
So that little triangle
help me kind of understand.

00:41:52.830 --> 00:41:54.120
So those three things,

00:41:54.120 --> 00:41:55.320
if they got out of whack,

00:41:55.320 --> 00:41:58.380
and it ceased to sort of
be a special operation.

00:41:58.380 --> 00:42:02.250
Anyway, so how do we get this
institution that we have now?

00:42:02.250 --> 00:42:04.420
And I think that's the biggest story

00:42:04.420 --> 00:42:08.760
of American Special Operations.

00:42:08.760 --> 00:42:10.740
We have this whole period

00:42:10.740 --> 00:42:11.820
where all of these sort

00:42:11.820 --> 00:42:14.370
of patchwork units are
operating independently.

00:42:14.370 --> 00:42:17.970
But now, as Colonel
Hooker was describing it,

00:42:17.970 --> 00:42:20.790
we've got this massive
industry of Special Operations

00:42:20.790 --> 00:42:23.130
that it's protected from the top,

00:42:23.130 --> 00:42:24.680
it's supported from the bottom.

00:42:26.730 --> 00:42:29.070
It's how America does everything,

00:42:29.070 --> 00:42:30.900
We take something small

00:42:30.900 --> 00:42:33.300
and we build it up into
a major corporation.

00:42:33.300 --> 00:42:34.653
And the fear now being,

00:42:35.760 --> 00:42:37.560
well, are we more bureaucratic?

00:42:37.560 --> 00:42:38.393
Probably.

00:42:39.540 --> 00:42:43.020
Are we less able to
respond to current threats?

00:42:43.020 --> 00:42:44.700
Possibly, yeah.

00:42:44.700 --> 00:42:46.800
But there's a reason that
we have that bureaucracy

00:42:46.800 --> 00:42:49.320
because there were all
of these other things

00:42:49.320 --> 00:42:51.900
that sort of forced us to
create that bureaucracy.

00:42:51.900 --> 00:42:53.490
So if we look at what happened in history,

00:42:53.490 --> 00:42:54.740
I think we can get there.

00:42:56.340 --> 00:42:57.600
Anyway, I could keep going,

00:42:57.600 --> 00:42:58.860
but I'll stop for now.

00:42:58.860 --> 00:42:59.910
- [Peter] Let me ask Colonel Collins

00:42:59.910 --> 00:43:01.380
because you've just
came back from Ukraine.

00:43:01.380 --> 00:43:02.213
I mean, obviously,

00:43:02.213 --> 00:43:03.540
one of the big successes

00:43:03.540 --> 00:43:05.310
in the Ukrainian war is Ukrainian SOF

00:43:05.310 --> 00:43:07.890
and Major General Repass
has been part of that

00:43:07.890 --> 00:43:10.830
who some people here may know.

00:43:10.830 --> 00:43:13.080
And now, of course, the
training is sort of being cut,

00:43:13.080 --> 00:43:14.700
done over FaceTime, essentially.

00:43:14.700 --> 00:43:16.830
I mean, it's not in theater.

00:43:16.830 --> 00:43:19.620
So why was that a success?

00:43:19.620 --> 00:43:24.000
And what is the likely
outcome going forward

00:43:24.000 --> 00:43:27.600
given the fact that it's not
hands on in the same way?

00:43:27.600 --> 00:43:28.860
- Yeah, first, I'll answer.

00:43:28.860 --> 00:43:30.150
I mean, I'll say it was a failure to deter

00:43:30.150 --> 00:43:32.430
and I think we could have done it,

00:43:32.430 --> 00:43:34.110
but there was no will in the US government

00:43:34.110 --> 00:43:35.280
to actually do it.

00:43:35.280 --> 00:43:37.290
So I'll spend a little bit of time on that

00:43:37.290 --> 00:43:38.550
if that's okay.

00:43:38.550 --> 00:43:40.950
Partly because there was no punishment

00:43:40.950 --> 00:43:43.620
for Russia in 2008 following
their invasion of Georgia,

00:43:43.620 --> 00:43:45.393
nothing in 2014,

00:43:45.393 --> 00:43:47.640
2015 following their initial invasion

00:43:47.640 --> 00:43:49.803
in illegal annexation into Ukraine.

00:43:50.790 --> 00:43:53.760
We did have a $300 million
roughly year investment

00:43:53.760 --> 00:43:54.810
that we were doing over there.

00:43:54.810 --> 00:43:56.220
And I spent two years going back and forth

00:43:56.220 --> 00:43:57.053
with General Abizaid

00:43:57.053 --> 00:43:58.320
so I understand,

00:43:58.320 --> 00:44:01.293
kind of saw the Ukrainians
evolve from 2016 to 2018.

00:44:03.690 --> 00:44:04.523
One of the things we did,

00:44:04.523 --> 00:44:06.240
and this was one of the mini signals is

00:44:06.240 --> 00:44:07.800
we asked both the SHAPE commander

00:44:07.800 --> 00:44:08.730
and we asked General Mattis

00:44:08.730 --> 00:44:09.990
to put a general officer

00:44:09.990 --> 00:44:12.630
in charge of the ground
of the effort in Ukraine.

00:44:12.630 --> 00:44:15.480
We had a 2 to 300-man joint
military training group

00:44:15.480 --> 00:44:17.080
train over there, training them.

00:44:18.090 --> 00:44:20.590
Half a dozen ministry
defense advisors over there,

00:44:21.540 --> 00:44:23.533
all these other kind of in and out things,

00:44:23.533 --> 00:44:26.040
and $350 million dollar aid package.

00:44:26.040 --> 00:44:28.710
You look at other places where
we've had this kind of thing.

00:44:28.710 --> 00:44:31.350
Pakistan, when we needed
to go into Pakistan

00:44:31.350 --> 00:44:32.490
and needed help from them,

00:44:32.490 --> 00:44:33.663
we put Mike Nagata.

00:44:35.700 --> 00:44:38.160
Israel, Saudi Arabia, some other places.

00:44:38.160 --> 00:44:39.120
When you need it,

00:44:39.120 --> 00:44:39.953
that's a signal, right?

00:44:39.953 --> 00:44:40.786
And it's a signal to Russia,

00:44:40.786 --> 00:44:41.970
you care about it.

00:44:41.970 --> 00:44:44.250
But the answer was we don't
have a general to give

00:44:44.250 --> 00:44:46.580
or admiral to give despite having 620

00:44:46.580 --> 00:44:47.880
in the Department of Defense.

00:44:47.880 --> 00:44:48.870
It meant we didn't care.

00:44:48.870 --> 00:44:50.040
And we were communicating to Russia,

00:44:50.040 --> 00:44:50.873
we cared.

00:44:50.873 --> 00:44:51.706
So bottom line,

00:44:51.706 --> 00:44:52.800
it was a failure to deter,

00:44:52.800 --> 00:44:55.200
and we are where we're at today.

00:44:55.200 --> 00:44:56.550
But yeah, the Ukrainians,

00:44:56.550 --> 00:44:58.170
I mean one of the things,

00:44:58.170 --> 00:44:59.400
everybody that we talked to over there

00:44:59.400 --> 00:45:00.233
over a two-year period,

00:45:00.233 --> 00:45:03.450
every single person said
they're not Iraqi or Afghani.

00:45:03.450 --> 00:45:05.700
If you look at what can the US do?

00:45:05.700 --> 00:45:07.363
What does the US not do?

00:45:07.363 --> 00:45:09.900
Well, we don't build a
military on scale in a nation

00:45:09.900 --> 00:45:11.910
where there's no political will to do it

00:45:11.910 --> 00:45:13.230
and no one's good at that, right?

00:45:13.230 --> 00:45:15.750
That's why it failed in
Afghanistan and Iraq.

00:45:15.750 --> 00:45:17.130
And that's why it worked in Ukraine,

00:45:17.130 --> 00:45:18.060
the political wolves behind it.

00:45:18.060 --> 00:45:19.200
They're the ones that wanted to reform

00:45:19.200 --> 00:45:20.820
and turn Western.

00:45:20.820 --> 00:45:21.990
If you go to the panel later today,

00:45:21.990 --> 00:45:24.480
you'll have somebody very critical of it.

00:45:24.480 --> 00:45:26.940
But I'll cut it shorter on there,

00:45:26.940 --> 00:45:29.100
but bottom line is the
SOF did a very good job

00:45:29.100 --> 00:45:31.356
training there as they
basically stood it up

00:45:31.356 --> 00:45:32.820
from scratch over there.

00:45:32.820 --> 00:45:36.810
And because they're kind
of better at building it

00:45:36.810 --> 00:45:38.250
than some of the other
commissional assets,

00:45:38.250 --> 00:45:41.250
so have built a pretty effective.

00:45:41.250 --> 00:45:42.450
And so, yeah,

00:45:42.450 --> 00:45:43.774
what's really dependent

00:45:43.774 --> 00:45:46.230
on Ukraine has continued western aid

00:45:46.230 --> 00:45:48.840
to support the Ukrainians

00:45:48.840 --> 00:45:50.310
because you've basically got a military

00:45:50.310 --> 00:45:51.330
that's got the advantage

00:45:51.330 --> 00:45:52.830
in every category except for mass.

00:45:52.830 --> 00:45:55.290
Mass is the one category
Russia has the advantage,

00:45:55.290 --> 00:45:56.880
and so they gotta kind of continue

00:45:56.880 --> 00:45:58.863
to replace those weapon systems.

00:45:58.863 --> 00:46:00.300
But yeah, they did a
good job of building it.

00:46:00.300 --> 00:46:01.710
- Because I think on Iraq, I mean,

00:46:01.710 --> 00:46:04.011
I would respectfully disagree,

00:46:04.011 --> 00:46:07.320
I think that with the Iraqi
counterterrorism service,

00:46:07.320 --> 00:46:10.650
it's one of the sort of
great unheralded successes.

00:46:10.650 --> 00:46:11.483
- Niche force,

00:46:11.483 --> 00:46:12.750
very good at building a niche force.

00:46:12.750 --> 00:46:13.583
- Okay.

00:46:13.583 --> 00:46:15.450
- We can't build a force on scale.

00:46:15.450 --> 00:46:16.500
- Gotcha.

00:46:16.500 --> 00:46:17.333
Okay.

00:46:17.333 --> 00:46:18.166
Any questions?

00:46:21.112 --> 00:46:24.029
(faintly speaking)

00:46:27.210 --> 00:46:28.230
- [Eric] Eric Donio,

00:46:28.230 --> 00:46:30.540
I'm an assault team leader
at Development Group.

00:46:30.540 --> 00:46:31.563
I have a question.

00:46:32.580 --> 00:46:33.413
Dr. Collins,

00:46:33.413 --> 00:46:38.130
based on what you just said
about the efforts in Ukraine,

00:46:38.130 --> 00:46:39.720
could you give some examples

00:46:39.720 --> 00:46:43.470
of what is the Ukrainian
special forces are doing

00:46:43.470 --> 00:46:48.180
that makes those operations
defined as special

00:46:48.180 --> 00:46:49.500
kind of according to the three tenets

00:46:49.500 --> 00:46:51.810
that Mr. Milligan spoke about?

00:46:51.810 --> 00:46:53.760
A lot of the stuff we see in the news

00:46:53.760 --> 00:46:55.290
and open source media seems like

00:46:55.290 --> 00:46:56.760
it's mainly conventional warfare

00:46:56.760 --> 00:46:58.710
that's taking place during the daytime.

00:46:59.730 --> 00:47:01.980
Would you be able to give some examples?

00:47:01.980 --> 00:47:03.115
- Yeah.

00:47:03.115 --> 00:47:04.770
I mean, just think of the
defensive key, for example.

00:47:04.770 --> 00:47:07.740
It's really hard to understand
the Ukrainian army coming in

00:47:07.740 --> 00:47:09.870
with a Western perspective
or a US perspective

00:47:09.870 --> 00:47:11.670
of how wars are fought.

00:47:11.670 --> 00:47:14.460
It's kind of like our inherent
distrust with our government.

00:47:14.460 --> 00:47:15.750
They kind of have that inherent...

00:47:15.750 --> 00:47:17.910
The Ukrainian population
has this inherent distrust

00:47:17.910 --> 00:47:19.470
with their military.

00:47:19.470 --> 00:47:21.360
And so going back to 2014,

00:47:21.360 --> 00:47:23.785
a massive number of volunteers went out

00:47:23.785 --> 00:47:26.100
to the east to really defend

00:47:26.100 --> 00:47:28.140
against the Russian-led separatist.

00:47:28.140 --> 00:47:30.540
So they have this really weird construct

00:47:30.540 --> 00:47:33.030
where you have this standing regular army,

00:47:33.030 --> 00:47:36.150
but then you still have
these massive volunteers

00:47:36.150 --> 00:47:36.983
from the defensive Kiev.

00:47:36.983 --> 00:47:40.560
You would have civilians
basically running the drones,

00:47:40.560 --> 00:47:42.660
establishing all the
named area of interest,

00:47:42.660 --> 00:47:44.460
like looking out there,

00:47:44.460 --> 00:47:45.810
talking right there with commanders.

00:47:45.810 --> 00:47:49.773
So where was this some of their
special forces role in this?

00:47:50.670 --> 00:47:51.810
Most of their force,

00:47:51.810 --> 00:47:53.250
they really thought the
attack was gonna come here.

00:47:53.250 --> 00:47:55.440
So they're really left with
a 72nd mechanized brigade

00:47:55.440 --> 00:47:57.780
kind of defending capitol.

00:47:57.780 --> 00:47:59.610
And they're really kind
of just at the edge

00:47:59.610 --> 00:48:01.230
of the city limits as far as they went.

00:48:01.230 --> 00:48:02.370
Pretty much everything forward

00:48:02.370 --> 00:48:04.110
to that was just
civilians carrying weapons

00:48:04.110 --> 00:48:07.620
and kind of step out there
kind of working with them,

00:48:07.620 --> 00:48:09.000
I mean, as an example, right?

00:48:09.000 --> 00:48:11.670
And so they're working with those guys,

00:48:11.670 --> 00:48:14.250
or they might have the RPG or an in-law,

00:48:14.250 --> 00:48:17.070
and then they're kind of
working with the ambush

00:48:17.070 --> 00:48:19.080
as they approach to the
conventional forces.

00:48:19.080 --> 00:48:21.363
So just kind of strange in that way.

00:48:23.250 --> 00:48:24.780
I don't think they've had
as much success really

00:48:24.780 --> 00:48:26.370
to kind of build in
that resistance network.

00:48:26.370 --> 00:48:28.294
And I think there's a couple reasons

00:48:28.294 --> 00:48:29.760
why I don't wanna get too much in Ukraine.

00:48:29.760 --> 00:48:30.600
We're gonna hit it later.

00:48:30.600 --> 00:48:32.150
We got a separate panel for it.

00:48:34.680 --> 00:48:37.650
Because one is a lot of
Ukrainians are just leaving.

00:48:37.650 --> 00:48:38.670
When the war comes to you,

00:48:38.670 --> 00:48:39.840
you get the hell out of there.

00:48:39.840 --> 00:48:40.800
I mean, most people leave cities

00:48:40.800 --> 00:48:42.662
and the ones that are left.

00:48:42.662 --> 00:48:44.340
So I think you haven't seen as
much resistance as you could.

00:48:44.340 --> 00:48:46.200
And part of it was that
those resistance network

00:48:46.200 --> 00:48:48.240
to be most effective had
to be set up in advance,

00:48:48.240 --> 00:48:49.770
and they weren't set up in advance.

00:48:49.770 --> 00:48:51.690
I mean, I did a video teleconference

00:48:51.690 --> 00:48:54.990
with kind of the forces
responsible for defending Kiev

00:48:54.990 --> 00:48:57.450
and this commander is basically,

00:48:57.450 --> 00:48:59.550
three weeks before the war started,

00:48:59.550 --> 00:49:01.500
no one believed that the
Russians were gonna invade

00:49:01.500 --> 00:49:02.940
so they really weren't prepared for it

00:49:02.940 --> 00:49:04.470
from that perspective.

00:49:04.470 --> 00:49:07.620
And then I think they're
doing some deep raids, too.

00:49:07.620 --> 00:49:09.420
But yeah, I mean,

00:49:09.420 --> 00:49:11.790
it's a weird construct the way they're...

00:49:11.790 --> 00:49:15.300
You would never have a
civilian come to the bunker

00:49:15.300 --> 00:49:16.500
on the second day of the war

00:49:16.500 --> 00:49:17.940
and come up with a plan to blow a dam

00:49:17.940 --> 00:49:19.597
and then just say,

00:49:19.597 --> 00:49:20.430
"Go.

00:49:20.430 --> 00:49:21.263
Here, we'll give an engineer,

00:49:21.263 --> 00:49:22.653
go to the ammo supply point.

00:49:23.597 --> 00:49:25.590
Pick up a ton of TNT or dynamite

00:49:25.590 --> 00:49:26.430
and blow the dam."

00:49:26.430 --> 00:49:27.480
It's just unheard of.

00:49:35.316 --> 00:49:37.350
- [Peter] In the absence
of other questions,

00:49:37.350 --> 00:49:39.060
do you have a question?

00:49:39.060 --> 00:49:39.893
Yeah.

00:49:39.893 --> 00:49:40.985
- [Will] Sure.

00:49:40.985 --> 00:49:41.880
- [Peter] Yeah.

00:49:41.880 --> 00:49:42.784
- [Will] Yeah.

00:49:42.784 --> 00:49:43.617
Hi, Will Myers,

00:49:43.617 --> 00:49:45.990
I'm an assault team leader
over at Gabled Stone.

00:49:45.990 --> 00:49:48.570
For Colonel Hooker and Dr. Collins,

00:49:48.570 --> 00:49:49.980
you kind of got on this as well.

00:49:49.980 --> 00:49:52.590
How do you resolve the
conflict in your mind

00:49:52.590 --> 00:49:55.290
if Special Operations
is a drain on the talent

00:49:55.290 --> 00:49:57.510
of the conventional force?

00:49:57.510 --> 00:49:58.890
How do you resolve the issue of the fact

00:49:58.890 --> 00:50:02.820
that if you're a conventional guy,

00:50:02.820 --> 00:50:05.190
it's not like you're gonna
stay in the conventional army

00:50:05.190 --> 00:50:06.510
if you don't go to SOF,

00:50:06.510 --> 00:50:08.260
maybe you just get out of the army?

00:50:09.240 --> 00:50:10.650
As we talk about it being a drain,

00:50:10.650 --> 00:50:12.750
maybe it's just an opportunity.

00:50:12.750 --> 00:50:13.980
someone like myself,

00:50:13.980 --> 00:50:15.450
I've been in the army 16 years,

00:50:15.450 --> 00:50:17.250
I wouldn't have been in the army 16 years

00:50:17.250 --> 00:50:19.800
if I had to stay conventional.

00:50:19.800 --> 00:50:21.513
Colonel, how do you address that?

00:50:23.070 --> 00:50:24.930
- Well, if you break that down,

00:50:24.930 --> 00:50:27.540
I think what you're really
saying is all these guys

00:50:27.540 --> 00:50:31.170
that migrate to the SOF community
are either gonna migrate

00:50:31.170 --> 00:50:32.160
to the SOF community

00:50:32.160 --> 00:50:34.140
or they're gonna get out.

00:50:34.140 --> 00:50:36.390
There's just no social
science behind that.

00:50:36.390 --> 00:50:37.770
There's no data behind that.

00:50:37.770 --> 00:50:39.960
That's all anecdotal stuff.

00:50:39.960 --> 00:50:41.550
The E-6 that go to selection

00:50:41.550 --> 00:50:43.800
and don't make selection
typically don't all get out

00:50:43.800 --> 00:50:44.633
of the army

00:50:44.633 --> 00:50:46.650
because they've got 10 years, 8 years,

00:50:46.650 --> 00:50:48.600
11 years invested.

00:50:48.600 --> 00:50:50.550
They're looking at
retirement down the road.

00:50:50.550 --> 00:50:52.980
So the community is very large.

00:50:52.980 --> 00:50:54.660
There's gonna be a suction pump effect

00:50:54.660 --> 00:50:58.170
that's gonna draw talented
or quality leaders

00:50:58.170 --> 00:50:59.730
into that side of the force.

00:50:59.730 --> 00:51:01.350
The question that we need to be asking

00:51:01.350 --> 00:51:05.670
at sort of the policy and
the strategy level is,

00:51:05.670 --> 00:51:10.110
is that where these folks
make the biggest contribution?

00:51:10.110 --> 00:51:11.698
At the end of the day,

00:51:11.698 --> 00:51:12.570
you don't win wars with commandos,

00:51:12.570 --> 00:51:13.683
you just don't.

00:51:14.520 --> 00:51:16.590
You'll win them probably
with heavy divisions

00:51:16.590 --> 00:51:18.150
and where their power.

00:51:18.150 --> 00:51:21.420
Now, as I said it in my
very opening remarks,

00:51:21.420 --> 00:51:22.890
there's a key role for SOF

00:51:22.890 --> 00:51:24.840
to play in the national security toolbox.

00:51:24.840 --> 00:51:25.923
No doubt about that.

00:51:27.090 --> 00:51:29.520
But my critique is that
we've become unbalanced.

00:51:29.520 --> 00:51:30.353
That's all.

00:51:30.353 --> 00:51:33.180
I'm just trying to promote this idea

00:51:33.180 --> 00:51:35.283
that we need to sort of rebalance.

00:51:36.210 --> 00:51:38.220
Let's get back into the joint game.

00:51:38.220 --> 00:51:41.100
Let's put all the tools under
the joint force commander

00:51:41.100 --> 00:51:44.130
and stop taking tools away
from the joint force commander

00:51:44.130 --> 00:51:46.530
so that we can integrate and
coordinate and synchronize

00:51:46.530 --> 00:51:49.170
and get better effects on the battlefield.

00:51:49.170 --> 00:51:50.003
Okay.

00:51:50.003 --> 00:51:51.600
The community's been fighting
against that for a long time.

00:51:51.600 --> 00:51:52.433
We need to get back,

00:51:52.433 --> 00:51:53.580
I think, to first principles

00:51:53.580 --> 00:51:54.870
and we'll be just fine.

00:51:54.870 --> 00:51:55.770
- [Peter] Colonel Hooker,

00:51:55.770 --> 00:51:57.930
you also quick sort of follow up,

00:51:57.930 --> 00:52:01.170
which is there may not be
72,000 people in Tampa,

00:52:01.170 --> 00:52:03.087
but there's certainly at least 60,000.

00:52:03.087 --> 00:52:05.051
And most of them are civilians, right?

00:52:05.051 --> 00:52:05.884
- 6,000.

00:52:05.884 --> 00:52:06.717
- [Peter] 6,000, okay.

00:52:06.717 --> 00:52:09.423
I mean, are there too
many civilians in SOCOM?

00:52:10.680 --> 00:52:13.260
- Well, I don't think I'm
qualified to answer that,

00:52:13.260 --> 00:52:15.160
but I can tell you how many there are.

00:52:17.122 --> 00:52:18.289
Of that 6,194,

00:52:19.808 --> 00:52:21.180
there are 2,000 civilians.

00:52:21.180 --> 00:52:22.860
So maybe, I don't know,

00:52:22.860 --> 00:52:23.730
you tell me.

00:52:23.730 --> 00:52:25.200
it's a lot of civilians.

00:52:25.200 --> 00:52:26.033
- I'll just jump in.

00:52:26.033 --> 00:52:26.866
I mean, Richard,

00:52:26.866 --> 00:52:27.800
you talked about data.

00:52:29.220 --> 00:52:30.450
I'd like to see the data

00:52:30.450 --> 00:52:33.120
because with as many 18x rays

00:52:33.120 --> 00:52:35.850
that special forces that come
right in from the street,

00:52:35.850 --> 00:52:37.410
as many of them that come into the force,

00:52:37.410 --> 00:52:40.530
I find it hard to believe
there's more NCOs going to SF now

00:52:40.530 --> 00:52:42.000
than there were 10 or 20 years ago.

00:52:42.000 --> 00:52:43.680
I don't see that that it's possible

00:52:43.680 --> 00:52:44.850
with the number perspective.

00:52:44.850 --> 00:52:46.200
- [Audience 1] If I could just interject

00:52:46.200 --> 00:52:48.630
a couple of data points,

00:52:48.630 --> 00:52:51.330
and I apologize I didn't
bring these specific numbers,

00:52:51.330 --> 00:52:54.450
but I can tell you that
we have collected data.

00:52:54.450 --> 00:52:59.280
And the significant majority of folks

00:52:59.280 --> 00:53:00.180
who go through selection

00:53:00.180 --> 00:53:03.480
and do not make it actually
do have a track record

00:53:03.480 --> 00:53:05.190
of going back to the regular forces

00:53:05.190 --> 00:53:07.110
and becoming some of the longest serving

00:53:07.110 --> 00:53:09.600
and most senior members of the NCO corps.

00:53:09.600 --> 00:53:10.770
So we do have data that, I think,

00:53:10.770 --> 00:53:11.940
shows almost the reverse,

00:53:11.940 --> 00:53:14.010
that we become pillars,

00:53:14.010 --> 00:53:14.880
sort of important pillars

00:53:14.880 --> 00:53:17.880
for the regular conventional force

00:53:17.880 --> 00:53:19.530
for those who don't end up making it.

00:53:19.530 --> 00:53:21.240
Not as many actually leave entirely,

00:53:21.240 --> 00:53:23.790
but they actually stay in at a higher rate

00:53:23.790 --> 00:53:26.280
and stay in for longer careers.

00:53:26.280 --> 00:53:29.040
I'll also point out that I
think the better comparison

00:53:29.040 --> 00:53:30.990
for the headquarters numbers

00:53:30.990 --> 00:53:34.890
in Tampa is if you look at what it takes

00:53:34.890 --> 00:53:37.380
for the Army, Navy, Air Force, or Marines,

00:53:37.380 --> 00:53:38.940
the military service side,

00:53:38.940 --> 00:53:42.090
because that headquarters is
both a military service side

00:53:42.090 --> 00:53:44.370
and AGCC operational side.

00:53:44.370 --> 00:53:45.630
There are two hats that are worn

00:53:45.630 --> 00:53:48.180
by that commander that is very unique,

00:53:48.180 --> 00:53:50.280
similar to the role that
actually Secretary Mayer,

00:53:50.280 --> 00:53:52.020
ASD SO/LIC wears

00:53:52.020 --> 00:53:55.170
as a operational policy
advisor for the secretary,

00:53:55.170 --> 00:53:57.480
as well as a service secretary like role.

00:53:57.480 --> 00:53:59.280
And so it is a little bit complicated

00:53:59.280 --> 00:54:01.410
to unpack those specifics.

00:54:01.410 --> 00:54:03.930
But I do think that some of the questions

00:54:03.930 --> 00:54:06.060
that, Colonel Hooker, that you
raised are really good ones

00:54:06.060 --> 00:54:07.560
for us to reflect on

00:54:07.560 --> 00:54:08.850
because I think for us

00:54:08.850 --> 00:54:10.710
to meet the challenge we set ourselves

00:54:10.710 --> 00:54:11.760
and continue to be better,

00:54:11.760 --> 00:54:14.250
we have to question our
underlying assumptions.

00:54:14.250 --> 00:54:17.160
And I think it's always fair

00:54:17.160 --> 00:54:18.690
to say what more can we do better?

00:54:18.690 --> 00:54:20.610
And are we giving the nation
the return on investment?

00:54:20.610 --> 00:54:24.420
And I do believe that we
are rebalancing the force

00:54:24.420 --> 00:54:27.600
to be more effective in competition spaces

00:54:27.600 --> 00:54:30.300
and to effectively help pathfind

00:54:30.300 --> 00:54:32.550
for the joint force in
certain ways in this space.

00:54:32.550 --> 00:54:33.720
And I think that's an important role

00:54:33.720 --> 00:54:36.660
that we like to play as pathfinders,

00:54:36.660 --> 00:54:39.540
but keeping that balanced with not failing

00:54:39.540 --> 00:54:40.710
in our No-Fail Missions,

00:54:40.710 --> 00:54:43.080
including those for CVO,

00:54:43.080 --> 00:54:44.530
I think, is really important.

00:54:46.823 --> 00:54:47.656
Like I said,

00:54:47.656 --> 00:54:48.489
it's not a debate,

00:54:48.489 --> 00:54:49.322
but I did wanna set the record straight

00:54:49.322 --> 00:54:50.490
that we do have some data about the impact

00:54:50.490 --> 00:54:51.330
on the conventional forces.

00:54:51.330 --> 00:54:54.510
And that's actually a little
bit counterintuitive, I think.

00:54:54.510 --> 00:54:55.343
Thanks.

00:54:59.970 --> 00:55:00.900
- [Michelle] Good morning, everyone.

00:55:00.900 --> 00:55:02.940
Thank you for being panelists today.

00:55:02.940 --> 00:55:04.110
My name's Michelle Poe.

00:55:04.110 --> 00:55:06.330
I am in the army under USASOC.

00:55:06.330 --> 00:55:07.950
I've been in for 18 years,

00:55:07.950 --> 00:55:11.070
and I'm a member of the PRK cohort.

00:55:11.070 --> 00:55:12.270
One class that we've been taking

00:55:12.270 --> 00:55:14.910
this semester is rethinking
Special Operations,

00:55:14.910 --> 00:55:19.740
and we've been examining how
SOCOM has 70,000 personnel

00:55:19.740 --> 00:55:22.800
across the globe to
conduct global operations

00:55:22.800 --> 00:55:27.180
and how certain elements of it are bloated

00:55:27.180 --> 00:55:28.623
and have too many people.

00:55:30.060 --> 00:55:35.060
And Colonel Hooker, as you
stated during your brief,

00:55:35.700 --> 00:55:37.440
basically once 9/11 kicked off,

00:55:37.440 --> 00:55:39.630
there was an ever-expanding number

00:55:39.630 --> 00:55:42.993
of elements being added to
Special Operations Forces.

00:55:43.830 --> 00:55:47.010
Now, that CT is winding
down after 20 years,

00:55:47.010 --> 00:55:48.210
and it may be time

00:55:48.210 --> 00:55:50.703
to start cutting away
those certain elements.

00:55:52.770 --> 00:55:55.650
One thing we've been
talking about in class is,

00:55:55.650 --> 00:55:59.190
is the government prepared to
cut down Special Operations

00:55:59.190 --> 00:56:02.220
to kind of direct it from the top-down?

00:56:02.220 --> 00:56:05.850
And how in an environment

00:56:05.850 --> 00:56:08.520
where we are not used to saying no

00:56:08.520 --> 00:56:12.600
or used to essentially saying, I'm sorry,

00:56:12.600 --> 00:56:15.030
but these hundred people
have to go back to the army,

00:56:15.030 --> 00:56:16.230
how do we do that?

00:56:16.230 --> 00:56:18.603
Is there any ideas from the panelists?

00:56:20.070 --> 00:56:21.600
- Well, you might have thought

00:56:21.600 --> 00:56:24.810
that as the GWOT began to wind down,

00:56:24.810 --> 00:56:26.100
and that really wasn't something

00:56:26.100 --> 00:56:28.225
that happened in 2020,

00:56:28.225 --> 00:56:30.450
that happened in like 2014 or so,

00:56:30.450 --> 00:56:31.350
you might have expected

00:56:31.350 --> 00:56:33.120
that just as the conventional force began

00:56:33.120 --> 00:56:34.170
to decline in size,

00:56:34.170 --> 00:56:36.900
so too with the SOF community.

00:56:36.900 --> 00:56:38.580
In fact, that has not been the case.

00:56:38.580 --> 00:56:41.310
So the additions that were made

00:56:41.310 --> 00:56:44.040
at the height of the GWOT
remain in place today.

00:56:44.040 --> 00:56:46.625
And year by year,

00:56:46.625 --> 00:56:49.440
the requests are actually more

00:56:49.440 --> 00:56:51.723
for augmentation than for reduction.

00:56:52.860 --> 00:56:55.860
So I would suggest that one of the reasons

00:56:55.860 --> 00:56:58.980
that the SOCOM staff
is, as large as it is,

00:56:58.980 --> 00:57:03.150
is not to make it more rapid,
and agile, and responsive,

00:57:03.150 --> 00:57:04.980
but to make it better able to contend

00:57:04.980 --> 00:57:07.140
and compete in this competition

00:57:07.140 --> 00:57:08.820
for resources inside the beltway,

00:57:08.820 --> 00:57:10.923
which it does very, very effectively.

00:57:11.760 --> 00:57:12.593
I mean,

00:57:12.593 --> 00:57:15.750
it's true that SOCOM has
some service-like functions,

00:57:15.750 --> 00:57:17.490
that's undeniable,

00:57:17.490 --> 00:57:20.820
but the Army staff is
tasked with looking after

00:57:20.820 --> 00:57:22.830
when you include the reserve component,

00:57:22.830 --> 00:57:24.360
almost 10 times as many people,

00:57:24.360 --> 00:57:26.370
more than 10 times as many people.

00:57:26.370 --> 00:57:29.010
So the idea that both staff
should be the same size seems

00:57:29.010 --> 00:57:32.160
to me is problematic.

00:57:32.160 --> 00:57:34.440
I don't expect to see a sharp reduction

00:57:34.440 --> 00:57:36.480
in the size of special forces,

00:57:36.480 --> 00:57:38.220
although I think it's...

00:57:38.220 --> 00:57:40.020
I mean, one of your foundational

00:57:40.020 --> 00:57:43.206
strategic documents makes the point

00:57:43.206 --> 00:57:44.039
over and over and over again

00:57:44.039 --> 00:57:46.260
that quality is more
important than quantity.

00:57:47.550 --> 00:57:48.840
I think it's unarguable

00:57:48.840 --> 00:57:51.060
that if you get twice as big,

00:57:51.060 --> 00:57:53.070
you're not gonna be able
to maintain the same level

00:57:53.070 --> 00:57:54.510
of quality as you had before.

00:57:54.510 --> 00:57:57.030
I just think that's unarguable

00:57:57.030 --> 00:57:59.100
and may contribute to
some of the bad things

00:57:59.100 --> 00:58:01.620
and disasters that we saw happened

00:58:01.620 --> 00:58:03.603
over the last 10 or 15 years.

00:58:04.680 --> 00:58:05.640
I don't think you're gonna get a lot

00:58:05.640 --> 00:58:07.710
of senior people agreeing with that,

00:58:07.710 --> 00:58:09.000
at least not publicly.

00:58:09.000 --> 00:58:11.700
But I think it's something
that's certainly true.

00:58:11.700 --> 00:58:15.033
And I don't expect a SOF
community to decline in size.

00:58:16.440 --> 00:58:18.960
I mean, I just don't
think it's a kind of issue

00:58:18.960 --> 00:58:21.390
that rises to the level of
the president's attention.

00:58:21.390 --> 00:58:23.310
And I think it would take
presidential intervention

00:58:23.310 --> 00:58:25.010
for something like that to happen.

00:58:26.550 --> 00:58:27.600
- State of your name.

00:58:29.568 --> 00:58:30.780
- [Jackie] Thanks, everyone.

00:58:30.780 --> 00:58:31.613
I'm Jackie Deal

00:58:31.613 --> 00:58:34.740
from long-term strategy
group in Washington D.C.

00:58:34.740 --> 00:58:36.060
And I was wondering

00:58:36.060 --> 00:58:37.620
if you guys could speak a little bit

00:58:37.620 --> 00:58:39.750
to how well we're doing

00:58:39.750 --> 00:58:42.330
or how well either the joint force

00:58:42.330 --> 00:58:43.830
or the SOF community is doing

00:58:43.830 --> 00:58:46.860
at understanding the PRC

00:58:46.860 --> 00:58:48.870
and the Chinese Communist Party

00:58:48.870 --> 00:58:52.770
as a competitor or as a opponent.

00:58:52.770 --> 00:58:54.030
It took a while, I think,

00:58:54.030 --> 00:58:57.900
for us to get up to
speed on terror networks

00:58:57.900 --> 00:59:00.090
and the radical Islamist threat.

00:59:00.090 --> 00:59:01.200
How would you say we're doing

00:59:01.200 --> 00:59:04.563
on understanding our peer foe today?

00:59:06.690 --> 00:59:08.310
- I'll take a jump at this.

00:59:08.310 --> 00:59:10.230
And this is from both me as the researcher

00:59:10.230 --> 00:59:12.003
and as an old intel guy.

00:59:13.800 --> 00:59:15.950
I think we're doing
about as well as we can

00:59:17.100 --> 00:59:18.240
as part of the research.

00:59:18.240 --> 00:59:19.680
Chinese military

00:59:19.680 --> 00:59:23.163
and educational
institutions publish a lot.

00:59:24.870 --> 00:59:26.340
They publish a lot.

00:59:26.340 --> 00:59:28.323
They publish a lot unclassified.

00:59:29.400 --> 00:59:31.140
We have pretty good intelligence access

00:59:31.140 --> 00:59:33.480
to a lot of the other communications

00:59:33.480 --> 00:59:35.160
at a lot of levels.

00:59:35.160 --> 00:59:38.403
It stops at the top
tier of the CCP, though.

00:59:39.570 --> 00:59:41.340
So what is the PLA

00:59:41.340 --> 00:59:44.790
or the PLA Navy likely to
do in a certain situation?

00:59:44.790 --> 00:59:48.300
I think we have SOF perspective

00:59:48.300 --> 00:59:49.920
and I think joint force perspective,

00:59:49.920 --> 00:59:51.360
a fairly good idea.

00:59:51.360 --> 00:59:53.280
But what's gonna happen with G

00:59:53.280 --> 00:59:55.473
and his six or seven
closer ones, we don't.

00:59:56.940 --> 00:59:59.040
That's Dave's opinion.

00:59:59.040 --> 01:00:00.180
Watch him really closely trying

01:00:00.180 --> 01:00:01.650
to answer our commander's question.

01:00:01.650 --> 01:00:04.473
How do we get G to say
not today to go to Taiwan?

01:00:05.820 --> 01:00:07.620
So there's a gap there.

01:00:07.620 --> 01:00:10.620
- Isn't that gap just maybe
baked into the situation,

01:00:10.620 --> 01:00:13.200
which is himself may not
know what he wants to do?

01:00:13.200 --> 01:00:14.033
- Absolutely.

01:00:17.910 --> 01:00:20.103
As an old S2 in the army,

01:00:21.030 --> 01:00:23.497
one time the armored
battalion commander asked me,

01:00:23.497 --> 01:00:25.439
"How come you can't tell me

01:00:25.439 --> 01:00:28.250
what the enemy infantry
battalions are gonna do

01:00:28.250 --> 01:00:30.210
at NTC in two days?

01:00:30.210 --> 01:00:33.390
I said, "Well, here's your
armored battalion commander,

01:00:33.390 --> 01:00:34.710
your buddy, you live next door to him.

01:00:34.710 --> 01:00:37.050
What's he gonna do in two days?"

01:00:37.050 --> 01:00:38.280
And he doesn't know.

01:00:38.280 --> 01:00:39.720
So yes, that is part of it.

01:00:39.720 --> 01:00:43.710
And also ESG is very powerful
and very well-protected

01:00:43.710 --> 01:00:45.690
and has, by their law,

01:00:45.690 --> 01:00:47.140
pretty much absolute control.

01:00:48.875 --> 01:00:50.130
I feel we know more about it

01:00:50.130 --> 01:00:53.490
than we did about the
inner workings of ISIS

01:00:53.490 --> 01:00:54.930
when they started rising.

01:00:54.930 --> 01:00:55.980
I think we know more about it

01:00:55.980 --> 01:00:58.230
than we did about the
inner workings Al-Qaeda

01:01:00.030 --> 01:01:01.170
shortly after 9/11

01:01:01.170 --> 01:01:02.523
because they write a lot.

01:01:03.660 --> 01:01:05.760
- One final comment from Dazi Logan.

01:01:05.760 --> 01:01:08.550
- [Dazi] Sorry, I don't wanna cut short

01:01:08.550 --> 01:01:11.010
that discussion on PRC
and our understanding,

01:01:11.010 --> 01:01:12.570
but I did wanna say,

01:01:12.570 --> 01:01:13.440
I think the other thing

01:01:13.440 --> 01:01:15.240
that it's important to keep in mind,

01:01:16.920 --> 01:01:18.210
again, I think that I don't think

01:01:18.210 --> 01:01:20.830
that we've grown too large
in SOF as we've shifted

01:01:21.720 --> 01:01:23.850
and I think the rules that we are playing

01:01:23.850 --> 01:01:25.110
and continue to play,

01:01:25.110 --> 01:01:27.210
but part of that comes down

01:01:27.210 --> 01:01:28.830
to a very basic fact,

01:01:28.830 --> 01:01:31.950
which is our most senior
national leaders keep turning

01:01:31.950 --> 01:01:33.090
to SOF.

01:01:33.090 --> 01:01:35.430
They keep asking SOF to do the missions.

01:01:35.430 --> 01:01:38.280
So if the president keeps
asking us to do these missions,

01:01:39.120 --> 01:01:40.920
we are using our force fully today.

01:01:40.920 --> 01:01:42.330
They are fully occupied.

01:01:42.330 --> 01:01:44.310
There's nobody sitting around
just thinking great thoughts

01:01:44.310 --> 01:01:46.367
except maybe the few of you
in this room (indistinct).

01:01:47.610 --> 01:01:48.443
But in general,

01:01:48.443 --> 01:01:50.310
the vast majority of our
forces is fully employed.

01:01:50.310 --> 01:01:52.980
And they keep being regularly employed

01:01:52.980 --> 01:01:55.080
for a spectrum of things that, arguably,

01:01:55.080 --> 01:01:55.913
in some cases,

01:01:55.913 --> 01:01:57.420
you could have conventional forces doing,

01:01:57.420 --> 01:01:58.410
but the senior leadership

01:01:58.410 --> 01:02:00.660
of this country doesn't appear willing

01:02:00.660 --> 01:02:03.210
to take that chance for whatever reason.

01:02:03.210 --> 01:02:05.580
And so I think that is
the fundamental factor

01:02:05.580 --> 01:02:06.927
that there are a lot of people

01:02:06.927 --> 01:02:09.153
who have raised the questions
that Colonel Hooker has is,

01:02:09.153 --> 01:02:10.530
have we grown too large?

01:02:10.530 --> 01:02:12.090
Can we maintain the quality?

01:02:12.090 --> 01:02:15.180
The flip side of that
is I think using a lot

01:02:15.180 --> 01:02:17.070
of what we've learned
over the last 20 years,

01:02:17.070 --> 01:02:19.020
we actually think that our assessment

01:02:19.020 --> 01:02:21.780
and selection process
have gotten much better.

01:02:21.780 --> 01:02:23.340
And so in some ways,

01:02:23.340 --> 01:02:25.980
our ability to maintain
quality has improved

01:02:25.980 --> 01:02:27.480
even as we have grown.

01:02:27.480 --> 01:02:30.180
And that is a little
bit of a counterbalance,

01:02:30.180 --> 01:02:31.770
but there clearly is a limit.

01:02:31.770 --> 01:02:34.200
And I think that's where we ask ourselves,

01:02:34.200 --> 01:02:35.700
are we being used in the missions

01:02:35.700 --> 01:02:37.350
that give the biggest return, again,

01:02:37.350 --> 01:02:38.880
on investment for the nation?

01:02:38.880 --> 01:02:41.010
Because where we are

01:02:41.010 --> 01:02:44.460
and arguably there are certain
places we would love to grow.

01:02:44.460 --> 01:02:46.470
We actually can't really
grow very effectively.

01:02:46.470 --> 01:02:48.000
I think there is a real limitation there

01:02:48.000 --> 01:02:49.230
that you identify.

01:02:49.230 --> 01:02:50.700
I don't think we hit it today,

01:02:50.700 --> 01:02:52.440
but I think we're very close.

01:02:52.440 --> 01:02:54.780
And so that actually becomes a frustration

01:02:54.780 --> 01:02:56.460
as national leaders ask us to do even more

01:02:56.460 --> 01:02:59.550
in certain spaces where we
haven't been able to grow.

01:02:59.550 --> 01:03:02.477
- Thanks to our well-informed
and lively panel.

01:03:02.477 --> 01:03:05.644
(audience applauding)

